   157	- *Setting.* Lists A, B of y-smooth integers generated without reference to N (e.g. sorted by ln). One merge serves
   158	  every k: all pairs with a·b ∈ ∪_k [kN, kN + h].
   159	- *H_eq.* For N-oblivious lists, a·b mod N is equidistributed: E[#pairs with ab ≥ N, ab mod N ≤ h] =
   160	  |A||B|(h + 1)/N·(1 + o(1)), **for every product range** [N, KN] (the windows have density (h+1)/N among integers
   161	  in any range of length ≫ N).
   162	- *Cost.* Generating and sorting the lists costs ≥ |A| + |B|. In the Prop SI regime (Kh ≪ √N ≈ min A) each a has at
   163	  most O(1) admissible b-intervals with an integer in them, findable by a 2-D lattice/continued-fraction step, so the
   164	  merge is O(|A| + |B| + hits) independently of K. (At toy sizes h > √N and our implementation pays per (a, k);
   165	  E1 records both counters.)
   166	- *Consequence.* R outputs need |A||B| ≥ RN/(h + 1), so cost ≥ 2√(RN/(h + 1)), i.e. per output
   167	  **≥ 2√(N/(R(h + 1)))**.
   168	  - L^{o(1)} per output iff R(h + 1) ≥ N·L^{−o(1)}.
   169	  - Prop SI uses R = L[1/3, κ + ν/(3κ)] and h = L[2/3, ν], so Rh = N^{o(1)} and the per-output cost is
   170	    N^{1/2 − o(1)}. The total, ≥ √(RN/h), is exponential.
   171	  - The amortisation over k is real (one list, all k) but worthless: what is amortised is the *search*, while the
   172	    *supply* of hits is fixed at (h + 1)/N per pair.
   173	- *Distinct outputs.* A smooth u in a window is hit once per factorisation u = ab with a ∈ A, b ∈ B, so distinct
   174	  outputs ≤ hits (E1: 2453 distinct of 2901 hits; codex's toy run: 264 of 4381). Multiplicity only lowers the
   175	  distinct yield.
   176	- sihard E3 tests the same two-list bound exactly (average over window positions) — see §8 once its note exists.
   177	
   178	### 2.4 k lists, Wagner, Schroeppel–Shamir, representations [MODEL]
   179	
   180	- Hits among k lists are ≤ ∏|L_i|·(h + 1)/N. Generic k-list algorithms in the low-solution regime (one solution per
   181	  ≈ N/h of the tuple space) find a hit at ≥ (N/h)^{1/2} cost (Schroeppel–Shamir time, BCJ-type exponents are also
   182	  constant fractions of log(N/h)).
   183	- Wagner's k-tree needs lists of size (N/h)^{1/(1+lg k)}, i.e. tuple space (N/h)^{k/(1+lg k)}, which exceeds the
   184	  available ≤ N^{1+o(1)} ordered factorisations for k ≥ 3 (smoothenum Prop W, extended here to products in any range:
   185	  the number of k-tuples of positive integers with product ≤ KN is ≤ KN(ln KN)^{k−1}).
   186	- **Representations** (Howgrave-Graham–Joux, BCJ) exploit many factorisations per solution. The average number of
   187	  ordered k-factorisations of a y-smooth u in the window is k^{u(1+o(1))}, u = x/ln y, i.e. L[2/3, o(1)] for fixed k
   188	  and L[2/3, O(1)] for k ≤ x (ρ_k(u) ≈ ρ(u)k^u). That is N^{o(1)}: it cannot remove an N^{Θ(1)} cost.
   189	
   190	## 3. Per-approach cost table
   191	
   192	Per-output cost of producing one y-smooth u ∈ I_k (any k ≤ K), y = L[1/3, κ], h = L[2/3, ν], R = L[1/3] outputs.
