   206	
   207	## 4. Approaches in detail
   208	
   209	### 4.1 Log-knapsack (approach 1)
   210	- u = ∏p_i^{e_i} ∈ I_k ⇔ Σe_i ln p_i ∈ [ln kN, ln kN + ε], ε ≈ h/(kN) = e^{−x + O(F_{2/3})}.
   211	- Exponent vectors: ‖e‖₁ ≤ x/ln 2; a typical witness has ≈ x/ln y = x^{2/3}(ln x)^{−2/3}/κ nonzero entries, mostly 1
   212	  (a constant fraction of witnesses is squarefree, HEURISTIC).
   213	- As a subset-sum instance: n = L[1/3] items, weight ≈ u = x^{2/3−o(1)} (sublinear), x bits of target precision.
   214	  Candidates Ψ(N, y) ≈ N·L[2/3, −2/(3κ)], so the solution count is only L[2/3, ν − 2/(3κ)] = 2^{o(x)}: a
   215	  **density-≈1 instance**, where every generic algorithm (MITM, Schroeppel–Shamir, HGJ/BCJ, Wagner) costs
   216	  2^{Θ(x)} = N^{Θ(1)}. The "high density" n ≫ x is illusory: the bound Σe_i ln p_i ≤ x leaves only N^{1−o(1)}
   217	  candidates.
   218	- Lattice: Prop CV. Exact CVP is needed, in dimension L[1/3].
   219	
   220	### 4.2 Meet-in-the-middle (approach 2): the amortisation analysis
   221	- Lemma MA. The per-pair hit probability (h + 1)/N is fixed by the target set, not by how many k one list serves.
   222	  Serving K multipliers enlarges the target set K-fold but also requires products K-fold larger; the density of the
   223	  windows among integers is (h + 1)/N at every scale.
   224	- **Precise amortised statement.** For R outputs the optimum is |A| = |B| = √(RN/(h+1)), cost 2√(RN/(h+1)) + R:
   225	  - per output: 2√(N/(R(h+1))) + 1;
   226	  - L^{o(1)} per output ⇔ R ≥ N/(h·L^{o(1)}), i.e. exponentially many outputs (unless h = N^{1−o(1)}, where the
   227	    residue v is no longer small and the method is Dixon-like, L[1/2, √2]);
   228	  - Prop SI needs only R = L[1/3, κ + ν/(3κ)]: the amortisation saves a factor √R = L[1/3] on N^{1/2}.
   229	- **Factory variant** (lists precomputed once for all N of a size): per-N cost |A| ≥ RN/(h|B|), so per-N L^{o(1)}
   230	  needs a precomputed table of size |B| ≈ N/h = N^{1−o(1)}. Exponential precomputation, comparable to a factor table:
   231	  irrelevant (assessment).
   232	- **Toy scale is misleading in MITM's favour.** On the toy L-path h ≈ N^{0.6–0.7}, so N/h is tiny. MITM beats the
   233	  interval sieve at every tested size (E2), and the o(1)-free model crossover is near 310 bits. Asymptotically the
   234	  per-output slope of MITM tends to 1/2 bit per bit of N, while the sieve's tends to 0.
   235	
   236	### 4.3 Free choice of k (approach 3)
   237	- The union of K windows has K·h·L[2/3, −2/(3κ)] smooth elements: K = L[1/3, C] is L[2/3, o(1)], so the existence
   238	  threshold ν > 2/(3κ) is unchanged.
   239	- Per-pair (MITM) and per-test (sieve, 2-D aim) success rates do not depend on K. A multi-target CVP gains at most a
   240	  factor K. So free k is worth L[1/3] at most, on top of costs of size L[2/3] or N^{Θ(1)}.
   241	- **Bohr-set check.** None of our methods lists u with u mod N ≤ L[2α], α < 1/3, or ≤ y. The one-sided Bohr-set
   242	  lister (suffmap E2) at α = 1/3 is just Prop SI with free k, and we do not solve it. The hit law (h + 1)/N per
   243	  oblivious pair shows the cost of every listed object grows as h shrinks, as it must.
   244	
   245	### 4.4 CRT / Boneh / Coppersmith (approach 4)
   246	- Boneh (STOC 2000) finds all z ∈ [X, X + H] with gcd(z, P) ≥ S when ln H ≲ (ln S)²/ln P. Certified outputs have a
   247	  known divisor d ≥ H (pilist Lemma S≥V, PROVED there). Here H = h, so d ≥ h, and Lemma RC applies to d: the untested
   248	  cofactor is ≥ N/(Kh) = N^{1−o(1)}.
   249	- **Full smoothness is impossible inside the decoding range.** For d ≈ N one needs ln P ≤ x²/ln h ≈
   250	  x^{4/3}(ln x)^{−1/3}/ν, so P involves only O(x/ln x) primes. The window then contains a T-smooth integer
   251	  (T = those primes) with probability ≈ h·L[2/3, 1/(3κ)]/N: exponentially small.
   252	- **Best partial use** (S ≈ h): about h·D(S)/S ≈ D(S) = L[1/3, 2ν/(3κ)] certified outputs per call (D(S) = number of
   253	  divisors of P near S), each with an untested cofactor ≈ N/h. Per output: L[2/3, 2/(3κ)]·L[1/3, −Θ(1)]. Larger S
   254	  loses L[2/3] in output count for an L[1/3] cofactor gain.
   255	- **Relation to Prop JR.** pilist Prop JR (L^{o(1)} batching gain) is proved in NFS scaling, and explicitly excludes
   256	  this congruence scaling, where one call gains g(d) = L[1/3, Θ(1)] (pilist critic M2). We confirm the L[1/3, Θ(1)]
   257	  gain here, and that it leaves the exponent 2/3 untouched. So Prop SI's regime differs from JR's only in the
   258	  lower-order gain.
   259	
   260	### 4.5 Own ideas (approach 5)
   261	- **Continued fractions / Farey / Stern–Brocot.** Convergents p/q of kN/s give s·p − kN·q small, a 2-D lattice of
   262	  determinant s: Lemma RC.
   263	- **Products of consecutive integers.** m(m+1)…(m+r−1) has smoothness probability ρ(u/r)^r = ρ(u)·r^u (better by
   264	  r^u), but every factor is tested and consecutive values are spaced ≈ rN^{1−1/r} ≫ h. For fixed k there is at most
   265	  one candidate, with probability ≈ h/(rN^{1−1/r}): exponential.
   266	- **Binomial coefficients, factorial ratios** C(n, j), n ≤ y: smooth by construction, but an N-oblivious family.
   267	  Products of them are a sub-semigroup of the y-smooth integers, so aiming them is the same knapsack (H_eq: hit
   268	  probability (h + 1)/N per member).
   269	- **Norm forms** (u = N(α), α a product of small primes of Z[i] or Z[√r]): the norm is multiplicative, so again the
   270	  knapsack over split primes. Units do not change the norm.
   271	- **Smooth-twin ladders.** Multiplying by ratios (j + 1)/j (j | u) steps ln u by ≈ 1/j ≥ 1/y. Precision e^{−x}
   272	  needs exponentially many combinations: a knapsack with small items, MITM-bounded.
   273	- **Square × smooth.** u = s²m with s arbitrary is enough for the linear algebra (s² has even exponents), but it is
   274	  Lemma RC with the constructed part s² ≤ Kh, or QS (m = 1, residue √N).
   275	- **NFS reading.** Prop SI is two-polynomial "NFS" with f₁ = X, f₂ = X − N and a skewed box (a ∈ [kN, kN + h],
   276	  b = k), with the f₁ side constructed. It is the degree-1 analogue of smoothenum's algebraic-side lister
   277	  (infimum 0.7631 there).
   278	- **Modulus-oblivious.** A constructor that sees only X = kN is a generic "smooth integer in [X, X + L_X[2/3]]"
   279	  algorithm. It would also speed up prime-field discrete-log relation collection (suffmap §4, row C1; not checked in
   280	  detail). That is further evidence that it is hard (assessment).
   281	
   282	### 4.6 Counting, sampling and dynamic programming (codex siconstruct_1, checked by us)
   283	- **Counting ⇒ constructing [PROVED].** An exact oracle for Ψ(b, y) − Ψ(a − 1, y) gives a constructor by binary
   284	  search: O(log h) calls find the least smooth integer in I_k, and the factorisation follows by bisecting on the
   285	  prime cutoff. So a cheap exact interval-count would suffice.
   286	- **No cheap count is known.** Subtracting two global estimates of size Xρ needs relative error below
   287	  h/X = e^{−x+O(F_{2/3})}; saddle-point or Perron-integral estimates give nothing of the kind. Exact counting is
   288	  lattice-point counting in a simplex of dimension π(y) = L[1/3] (Barvinok-type algorithms are polynomial only in
   289	  fixed dimension); Bernstein's exact counting uses a meet-in-the-middle whose cost is governed by the two generated
   290	  sets (codex: Bernstein, "Enumerating and counting smooth integers", §3, Algorithm 3). For an *explicit arbitrary*
   291	  set of primes the existence question is NP-hard by sihard's X3C reduction (see sihard's note), so a fast counter
   292	  would have to use that the factor base is an initial segment of the primes.
   293	- **Sampling.** Uniform or approximate generators of factored smooth numbers (Bach; Kalai; Bach–Sorenson,
   294	  arXiv 2006.07445 Thms 3.1, 5.1–5.3) sample globally. Conditioning on I_k costs ≈ N/h trials, and their
   295	  enumeration order is lexicographic in the prime factors, not by size, so "select the rank near Ψ(kN, y)" does not
   296	  select an integer near kN.
   297	- **Log-grid dynamic programming.** A DP over rounded logarithms with per-item error η and s items needs sη ≲ h/X,
   298	  hence ≥ (ln X)/η = N^{1−o(1)} bins (codex; for that explicit-grid class only).
   299	- **Existence theorems** are far from this scale: the best all-interval results need y ≥ exp(C(ln X)^{2/3}(ln ln X)^{4/3})
   300	  (Jain, arXiv 2502.10530, Thms 1.1–1.2, as reported by codex; not checked by us), while here ln y ≍ (ln X)^{1/3}.
   301	
   302	## 5. Total factoring cost and heuristic R
   303	
   304	- Every constructor we found has per-output cost ≥ L[2/3]; plugged into Prop SI that gives L[2/3]. Used directly as
   305	  relation generators (u and the residue both tested, total random size N^{1−o(1)}), they are at best Dixon-like,
   306	  L[1/2, √2] (Theorem SS), which is worse than QS.
   307	- MITM: total ≥ √(RN/h), exponential, on top of linear algebra L[1/3, 2κ].
   308	- **Diversity (codex).** Multiplying one relation u₀ ≡ v₀ by a smooth s gives su₀ ≡ sv₀, a new valid output with the
   309	  same exponent-difference vector: worthless. A constructor must produce L[1/3, κ] *independent* relations, and if one
   310	  output per multiplier is used, the multiplier range must have C ≥ κ + ν/(3κ).
   311	- **Fruitfulness (heuristic R).** Not tested here: no constructor reached the regime where it matters. MITM outputs
   312	  are N-oblivious products whose residues are determined by (a·b mod N); sicheck's Prop B (Blum N: fruitfulness is a
   313	  parity condition computable from N) applies to any constructor, and sicheck's `beta0` example shows a constructor
   314	  can be useless while producing valid relations.
   315	
