   349	  h grows like N^{0.57+}, so the sieve's cost grows faster per bit. Asymptotically the slopes tend to 1/2 and 0:
   350	  the model crossover is ≈ 310 bits (MITM 94.2 vs sieve 93.0 log₂ per output at 320 bits).
   351	
   352	### E3. 2-D aim, constructed s and tested cofactor (40/48/56 bits, K = 16, 64 values of s, ≤ 2048 offsets per (s, k))
   353	- P3b (Lemma RC(i)): every tested cofactor has bit length ≥ that of N/(2S0): PASS (all 12 cells, equality).
   354	- **P3a: FAIL as registered for the small-s cells** (S0 = 2^{⌊lg h⌋/2}): distinct outputs / Σδ_HT(m) = 0.38–0.75.
   355	  PASS for the large-s cells (S0 = h/16): 0.83–1.24.
   356	  - Cause (design flaw): with small s, the 64 values of s scan the same short stretches [kN, kN + 2048s], and one
   357	    smooth u is found through several s (multiplicity 1.6–2.4 at 40–48 bits).
   358	  - POST-HOC diagnostic (`diag_e3.py`, same seeded lists): *successful tests with multiplicity* / Σδ_HT(m) = pooled
   359	    5549/5815 = 0.954, cells 0.49–1.25. So each cofactor is smooth like a random integer of its size.
   360	- P3c (tests per output vs brute force): large-s cells 33–140 tests per output against 6.3e5–4.9e7 for the sieve (H-T model at KN/2). At
   361	  toy sizes s ≈ h/16 leaves a cofactor of only 16–24 bits. That is the toy artefact h = N^{0.6+}. At Prop SI scale
   362	  the cofactor is ≥ N/(Kh) = N^{1−o(1)} (on this path with K = L[1/3, 1]: ≈ 67% of the bits at 1024 bits, 79% at 4096).
   363	
   364	**What the experiments show.** The laws behind the negative analysis hold at toy size: (h + 1)/N per oblivious pair
   365	regardless of K (E1, E2), and smoothness of the tested part exactly at its size (E3). They do not and cannot show
   366	asymptotic statements. MITM and 2-D aim are excellent at toy sizes only because toy h is a large power of N.
   367	
   368	## 7. Deviations and post-hoc items
   369	- P2c and P3a failed as registered; both causes are design errors in the *comparison*, explained above, with
   370	  post-hoc corrected comparisons labelled POST-HOC.
   371	- `diag_e3.py` is an unregistered diagnostic, run after E3.
   372	
   373	## 8. Reviews
   374	- **Codex blind round** (`codex/siconstruct_1.out`, GPT-6.1-sol, xhigh, run before our analysis was written down).
   375	  It reached the same verdict independently: interval sieve L[2/3, 2/(3κ)]; construct-one-factor-and-test has a
   376	  cofactor ≥ N/h (its version of Lemma RC); two explicit sorted lists cost N^{1/2+o(1)} per output for
   377	  L[1/3, O(1)] outputs (its version of Lemma MA, with the exact sorted-list accounting Õ(m + n + Km + H));
   378	  Wagner's tree violates M^r ≤ KN for r ≥ 4; Boneh's and GSS's recovery conditions fail by a huge factor; exact
   379	  CVP costs 2^{m+o(m)} (ADS 2015, Cor. 6.5, checked by codex), m = L[1/3, κ]. Items we adopted:
   380	  - a *binary* embedding e_p = Σ2^j z_{p,j} that enforces e ≥ 0, for which exact CVP solves Prop SI and
   381	    γ² ≤ 1 + 3/(m + 1) suffices (an alternative to Prop CV that handles non-squarefree outputs; dimension
   382	    m = O(π(y) ln x));
   383	  - the fixed-κ threshold τ < 0.6905 (§1);
   384	  - the diversity caveat (§5);
   385	  - the counting-to-search reduction, the precision obstruction for approximate counts, the log-grid DP bound and the
   386	    Jain and Bach–Sorenson references (§4.6).
   387	  Codex labels general non-existence UNVERIFIED, as we do: these are barriers for named method classes.
   388	
