notes/suffmap_results.md-28-## 0. Verdict
notes/suffmap_results.md-29-
notes/suffmap_results.md-30-**Plain language.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-31-- We found **no sufficient condition that is both plausible and gives an exponent below 1/3.** Every sufficient
notes/suffmap_results.md-32-  condition on the map is one of three kinds:
notes/suffmap_results.md-33-  1. **as strong as polynomial-time factoring in disguise**, and usually also a breakthrough elsewhere (prime-field
notes/suffmap_results.md-34-     discrete logarithms, algebraic circuit complexity, quantum simulation);
notes/suffmap_results.md-35-  2. **only an L[1/3] constant**, some of them tied to concrete, attackable problems;
notes/suffmap_results.md-36-  3. **already refuted for typical N**, or a model in which the target is absent under the standard counting
notes/suffmap_results.md-37-     heuristics.
notes/suffmap_results.md-38-- **The organising theorem is the size–entropy law (Theorem SS).** For a method that *samples* candidates and waits for
notes/suffmap_results.md-39-  them to be smooth over *one shared factor base*, with every examined candidate tested in full (model S, §1.1),
notes/suffmap_results.md-40-  everything is decided by one function λ(C): the smallest total log-size of the smoothness integers when e^C
notes/suffmap_results.md-41-  candidates are examined. The cost is e^{C*}, where C* is the least C with C² ≥ 2λ(C)·log λ(C). For a power
notes/suffmap_results.md-42-  law λ = A·x^a(log x)^{1−a−b}C^b this gives **α = a/(2 − b)** and **c^{2−b} = 4αA**. The formula reproduces Dixon √2,
notes/suffmap_results.md-43-  QS 1, GNFS (64/9)^{1/3}, SNFS (32/9)^{1/3} and t-dimensional sieving exactly (§5). An L[α] sampling method needs
notes/suffmap_results.md:44:  candidates of log-size σ = 2α in the exponent: **σ = 1/2 for α = 1/4, 2/5 for 1/5, 1/5 for 1/10**, and
notes/suffmap_results.md-45-  λ = O((log x)²/log log x) for polynomial time. In this model faster linear algebra (any ω ≤ 2) does not move the
notes/suffmap_results.md-46-  cost.
notes/suffmap_results.md-47-- **Scope of model S.** MNFS (shared rational filtering, then V fields on the survivors; L[β_a + v] columns), unions
notes/suffmap_results.md-48-  of many polynomial pairs, and the factory lie *outside* S. That is why MNFS reaches 1.9019 < 1.9230 and feels ω.
notes/suffmap_results.md-49-  Theorem SS is a statement about S, not about all relation methods.
notes/suffmap_results.md-50-- **Listing escapes the size law** (Theorem LS). If smooth objects are *listed* rather than waited for, size enters only
notes/suffmap_results.md-51-  through existence (box sizes), and the cost is the per-output listing cost. A complete search oracle gives a lister
notes/suffmap_results.md-52-  with ≤ 2K + 1 calls (Lemma SL, PROVED). A *promise* constructor (one that only works on intervals guaranteed to contain
notes/suffmap_results.md-53-  a smooth number) is worth exactly one output per tile of its guaranteed length (Prop PC, PROVED).
notes/suffmap_results.md:54:- **The cleanest new sufficient condition** is about constructing smooth numbers in a given short interval (Prop SI).
notes/suffmap_results.md-55-  Suppose a y-smooth integer can be constructed in polynomial time in every interval [kN, kN + h], with
notes/suffmap_results.md:56:  h = L[2/3, ν], y = L[1/3, κ] and ν > 2/(3κ). Then N factors in **L[1/3, (16/9)^{1/3} + ε] = L[1/3, 1.2114 + ε]**
notes/suffmap_results.md-57-  for every ε > 0 (HEURISTIC: the residue behaves randomly and the dependencies are fruitful). That is below MNFS 1.9019 and SNFS 1.526, with no number field at all. Single intervals can
notes/suffmap_results.md-58-  never beat exponent 1/3 (model-PROVED: max(α, 1 − 2α)). The sparse-set version, smooth numbers in the union of
notes/suffmap_results.md-59-  windows around many multiples of N (a Bohr set), is a *different* hypothesis (an oracle on unions of windows). With
notes/suffmap_results.md-60-  windows of width ≤ y it is the doubly lister E3 and gives heuristic polynomial time, even from a promise constructor.
notes/suffmap_results.md:61:  Prop SI itself is a one-sided form of Schnorr's target. No prior statement of the 1.2114 reduction was found by us, and codex found the
notes/suffmap_results.md-62-  same constant independently.
notes/suffmap_results.md-63-
notes/suffmap_results.md-64-**The map in one table** (details §2, high-profile verdicts §3, ranking §4).
notes/suffmap_results.md-65-
notes/suffmap_results.md-66-| Tier | Sufficient condition (short) | Gives | Believed |
notes/suffmap_results.md-67-|---|---|---|---|
notes/suffmap_results.md:68:| E | sampling family with C* = O(F_α), α < 1/3 (rank-r aggregate, toric) | L[α], formally 1/4 | false for typical N (G_r) |
notes/suffmap_results.md-69-| E | one-sided Bohr-set lister (smooth u, u mod N ≤ L[2α]) at L[α, O(1)] per output | L[α] (with u mod N ≤ y it is the doubly lister: poly time) | false |
notes/suffmap_results.md-70-| E | doubly-smooth lister / S-unit enumeration / relation-lattice vectors / Cayley-graph navigation | heuristic poly time (navigation to arbitrary targets: rigorous randomized poly time) | false; in coset form it would also give prime-field DL |
notes/suffmap_results.md-71-| E | short SLPs for Pochhammer polynomials (⇐ permanent in constant-free VP) | factoring ∈ P/poly, **rigorous** | false (τ-conjecture) |
