notes/suffmap_results.md-82-
notes/suffmap_results.md-83-**High-profile verdicts (§3).**
notes/suffmap_results.md-84-- Matomäki–Radziwiłł / Harper-type existence of smooth numbers in short intervals: **irrelevant to speed** (no
notes/suffmap_results.md-85-  algorithm, used by none).
notes/suffmap_results.md:86:  They become the *existence half* of the promise-constructor route (Prop PC, Prop SI) only at the scale
notes/suffmap_results.md-87-  y = L_X[1/3], h = L_X[2/3], far beyond current results.
notes/suffmap_results.md:88:- Deterministic construction of a smooth number in a given interval: **sufficient for L[1/3, 1.2114]** at the Prop SI
notes/suffmap_results.md-89-  scale, and **for heuristic polynomial time** in the Bohr-set form.
notes/suffmap_results.md-90-- Polymath4 (primes): **unrelated**.
notes/suffmap_results.md-91-- Sum-product: **unrelated**.
notes/suffmap_results.md-92-- Expansion: existence of short paths is **unrelated**; constructive navigation is **sufficient** (= doubly listing).
notes/suffmap_results.md-93-- Small-discriminant fields with a root mod N: **constant only**, and the height form is refuted for almost all N.
notes/suffmap_results.md-94-- abc and S-unit counting: **unrelated**; algorithmic S-unit solving is **sufficient**.
notes/suffmap_results.md-95-- Approximate CVP in prime lattices: a *method* for the listing targets; whether any approximation factor suffices is
notes/suffmap_results.md-96-  schnorrcvp's question.
notes/suffmap_results.md-97-- Coppersmith: **exponential regime only**.
notes/suffmap_results.md-98-- CRT list decoding beyond Johnson: **constant only**, unless doubly.
notes/suffmap_results.md-99-- Sparse linear algebra: **constant only**.
notes/suffmap_results.md-100-- Factorial / Pochhammer straight-line programs: **sufficient (nonuniform P), and a landmark either way**.
notes/suffmap_results.md-101-
notes/suffmap_results.md-102-**Ranking (§4).**
notes/suffmap_results.md:103:- *Most worth attacking (assessment; codex prefers C2 or C5 for clearer interfaces):* the Prop SI problem. Construct, or prove hard in a model, L[1/3]-smooth integers in intervals
notes/suffmap_results.md-104-  of length L[2/3] near multiples of N. It is concrete, no barrier beyond method classes is known, it is
notes/suffmap_results.md-105-  modulus-generic, and a solution would cut the factoring constant from 1.90 to 1.21.
notes/suffmap_results.md-106-- *Landmark either way:* the straight-line complexity of factorials / Pochhammer polynomials. A proof of easiness gives
notes/suffmap_results.md-107-  nonuniform polynomial-time factoring. A proof of hardness, quantified over power-of-two multiples, gives a
notes/suffmap_results.md-108-  constant-free permanent lower bound (Bürgisser 2009).
notes/suffmap_results.md-109-- *Highest-profile direct exponent target:* the doubly-smooth / Bohr-set lister (Schnorr's target). Its consequence for
notes/suffmap_results.md-110-  prime-field DL (Prop DLT) makes it implausible.
notes/suffmap_results.md-111-
notes/suffmap_results.md-112----
notes/suffmap_results.md-113-
notes/suffmap_results.md-114-## 1. Two principles
notes/suffmap_results.md-115-
notes/suffmap_results.md-116-### 1.1 Models
notes/suffmap_results.md-117-
notes/suffmap_results.md-118-**Sampling model S.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-119-- From N, the algorithm generates candidates z from a family 𝓕 and *examines* them, paying ≥ 1 each.
notes/suffmap_results.md-120-- Each z carries k = O(1) nonzero smoothness integers n₁(z), …, n_k(z), of total log-size λ(z) = Σ log|n_i(z)|.
notes/suffmap_results.md-121-- Each z also carries a multiplicative link to Z/N: congruences of products, or ring maps as in resbarrier's LOM.
notes/suffmap_results.md-122-  F₂-dependencies among B-smooth candidates therefore give X² ≡ Y² (mod N).
notes/suffmap_results.md-123-- All candidates share **one factor base** of π(B)(1 + o(1)) columns, and each examined candidate is tested on *all*
notes/suffmap_results.md-124-  its k integers, at cost ≥ 1. There is no cheaper pre-filtering on one component.
notes/suffmap_results.md-125-- Linear algebra on K columns costs K^ω.
notes/suffmap_results.md-126-
notes/suffmap_results.md-127-**What S excludes (proof critic M1).**
notes/suffmap_results.md-128-- MNFS: one shared rational side is sieved over E² points, and only its survivors are tested in V fields. The cost is
notes/suffmap_results.md-129-  E²(1 + V·L[−r]), not E²·V, and there are L[β_a + v] columns.
notes/suffmap_results.md-130-- Unions of M polynomial pairs: entropy 2 log E + log M with M separate factor bases. Their law is
notes/suffmap_results.md-131-  2x/D + D(C − log M)/2 + O(log M), better than 2√(xC) for one pair.
notes/suffmap_results.md-132-- The factory, which uses precomputation.
notes/suffmap_results.md-133-- Listing (§1.3).
notes/suffmap_results.md-134-Theorem SS therefore does not cover the best known algorithm (MNFS). Its role is to say exactly what a *single-family*
notes/suffmap_results.md-135-sampling method must achieve.
notes/suffmap_results.md-136-
notes/suffmap_results.md-137-**Heuristic H_S.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-138-- An examined z is B-smooth with probability ρ(λ(z)/log B)^{1+o(1)}, where −log ρ(u) = (1 + o(1))·u log u.
notes/suffmap_results.md-139-- This is per examined candidate, independent of how z was selected. That clause is a **strengthening** of heurnfs H,
notes/suffmap_results.md-140-  which only lets joint events multiply and so allows conditioning. The clause fails for candidates pre-filtered on one
notes/suffmap_results.md-141-  component, which is why such filtering is excluded from S.
notes/suffmap_results.md-142-- Smoothness tests and candidate generation cost L^{o(1)}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-143-
notes/suffmap_results.md-144-**Heuristic R.** π(B)·L^{±o(1)} relations are needed and suffice, and the dependencies are fruitful. For random-like
notes/suffmap_results.md-145-relations, "needed" is Croot–Granville–Pemantle–Tetali 2012 (up to a constant). For structured families it is an
notes/suffmap_results.md-146-assumption.
notes/suffmap_results.md-147-
notes/suffmap_results.md-148-**Size–entropy profile.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-149-- C_𝓕(λ) := log #{z ∈ 𝓕 : λ(z) ≤ λ}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-150-- The *law* λ(C) is its inverse: the smallest λ such that e^C candidates of size ≤ λ exist.
notes/suffmap_results.md-151-- Example: two-polynomial NFS under G has λ(C) = 2√(xC) (heurnfs B1 with C = 2 log E). SNFS has √(2xC).
notes/suffmap_results.md-152-
notes/suffmap_results.md-153-**Why k = O(1) matters.** If a candidate's smoothness integers are many small pieces (k ≍ u), then ∏ρ(λ_i/μ) is no
notes/suffmap_results.md-154-longer ρ(λ/μ). The extreme is "smooth by construction": a product of factor-base elements. That is the *listing* regime
notes/suffmap_results.md-155-of §1.3, not a counterexample to §1.2. systeq §6, ptesplit and nonexact live there.
notes/suffmap_results.md-156-
notes/suffmap_results.md-157-### 1.2 Theorem SS (sampling) [model-PROVED in S + H_S + R; KNOWN in substance]
notes/suffmap_results.md-158-
notes/suffmap_results.md-159-Write T(λ) := √(2λ log λ).
notes/suffmap_results.md-160-
notes/suffmap_results.md-161-**(a) Fixed size [KNOWN].** If every examined candidate has size λ, the cost is exp((1 + o(1))T(λ)). In Pomerance's
notes/suffmap_results.md-162-notation this is L(X)^{√2+o(1)} with X = e^λ, L(X) = exp(√(log X log log X)) (Pomerance 1982; 2008 survey "Smooth numbers
notes/suffmap_results.md-163-and the quadratic sieve", MSRI Publ. 44). The optimum is attained at B = exp(T(λ)/2), with e^{T(λ)} candidates.
notes/suffmap_results.md-164-
notes/suffmap_results.md-165-**(b) L-units.** If λ = s·F_{2α}, the cost is L[α, 2√(αs)], with factor base L[α, √(αs)] and L[α, 2√(αs)] candidates.
notes/suffmap_results.md-166-Equivalently: **L[α, c] needs candidates of total log-size ≤ (c²/(4α))·F_{2α}**, i.e. σ = 2α.
notes/suffmap_results.md-167-
notes/suffmap_results.md-168-**(c) Size–entropy law.** Let C*_δ := inf{C : C² ≥ (1 − δ)·2λ(C) log λ(C)} and C* := C*_0. Two regularity
notes/suffmap_results.md-169-assumptions are used:
notes/suffmap_results.md-170-- *Levels:* λ_max ≤ exp(x^{O(1)}), so that (1+ε)-adic size levels number L^{o(1)}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-171-- *Stability:* C*_δ = (1 + o(1))C* as δ → 0, and λ((1 + o(1))C*) = (1 + o(1))λ(C*). This holds for every regularly
notes/suffmap_results.md-172-  varying law, in particular every law in the catalogue.
notes/suffmap_results.md-173-- Without stability the theorem fails: a step profile with λ = λ₀ up to just below T(λ₀), then huge, has C* huge while
notes/suffmap_results.md-174-  cost ≈ e^{T(λ₀)} cannot be excluded under ρ^{1+o(1)} (proof critic m1).
notes/suffmap_results.md-175-- (lower bound) Every algorithm in model S costs ≥ exp((1 − o(1))C*). This needs **no** assumption on linear algebra,
notes/suffmap_results.md-176-  but it does use heuristic R (≈ π(B) relations are *needed*). H_S alone does not imply that: a generator could supply
notes/suffmap_results.md-177-  relations with extra structure (codex). For *raw independent uniform integers* there are theorems on the
notes/suffmap_results.md-178-  first-square-product threshold: Croot–Granville–Pemantle–Tetali 2012; Balister–Bollobás–Morris (arXiv 1608.03857,
notes/suffmap_results.md-179-  Thm 1.1) give T(X) ∼ e^{−γ}·min_y π(y)X/Ψ(X, y). Their model allows dependencies through primes outside a prescribed
notes/suffmap_results.md-180-  base, so they support R only as an analogy. For structured families R is an assumption.
notes/suffmap_results.md-181-- (upper bound) If 𝓕 is enumerable at L^{o(1)} per candidate, H_S holds as an equality, and ω ≤ 2, the cost is
notes/suffmap_results.md-182-  ≤ exp((1 + o(1))C*). This needs the population to extend a factor e^{o(C*)} beyond the threshold, which stability
notes/suffmap_results.md-183-  guarantees, and yield concentration, which follows from H_S's independence by Chernoff. Without stability the
notes/suffmap_results.md-184-  general statement is unjustified (codex suffmap_2 #4). The clean restricted theorem is for regularly varying laws,
notes/suffmap_results.md-185-  in particular the power laws of (d).
notes/suffmap_results.md-186-- So for single-family sampling, **ω < 2 does not help**. This agrees with heurnfs (GNFS stays at 1.9230 at ω = 1.5).
notes/suffmap_results.md-187-  MNFS gains from ω < 2 only because its V fields have different column structures.
notes/suffmap_results.md-188-
notes/suffmap_results.md-189-**(d) Power laws.** For λ(C) = A·x^a(log x)^{1−a−b}·C^b with a > 0, 0 ≤ b < 2 and a < 2 − b, so that 0 < α < 1:
notes/suffmap_results.md-190-
notes/suffmap_results.md-191-  C* = (c + o(1))F_α,   α = a/(2 − b),   c^{2−b} = 4αA.
notes/suffmap_results.md-192-
notes/suffmap_results.md-193-The log-power 1 − a − b is the only one for which the law is L-homogeneous. Other log-powers move the constant to 0 or
notes/suffmap_results.md-194-∞ at the same α. If b ≥ 2 there is no subexponential solution.
notes/suffmap_results.md-195-
notes/suffmap_results.md-196-**(e) Polynomial and quasi-polynomial time.** Here generation and tests must cost poly(x), not L^{o(1)}, and the
notes/suffmap_results.md-197-level argument loses an additive O(log x) in C (proof critic m2). With these changes:
notes/suffmap_results.md-198-- C* = O(log x) iff λ(C*) = O((log x)²/log log x).
notes/suffmap_results.md-199-- λ = (log x)^k gives exp(O((log x)^{k/2}(log log x)^{1/2})), which is quasi-polynomial.
notes/suffmap_results.md-200-
notes/suffmap_results.md-201-**Proof.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-202-1. *Optimal B for fixed λ.* With μ = log B and u = λ/μ, the relations needed are B, and each examined candidate succeeds
notes/suffmap_results.md-203-   with probability ρ(u). So the number of candidates is ≥ exp(μ + u log u)·(1 − o(1)).
notes/suffmap_results.md-204-   g(μ) := μ + (λ/μ)log(λ/μ) is minimised at μ₀ = √(λ log λ/2)(1 + o(1)). There log u = (½ + o(1))log λ, so
notes/suffmap_results.md-205-   u log u = μ₀(1 + o(1)) and g(μ₀) = 2μ₀ = T(λ)(1 + o(1)).
notes/suffmap_results.md-206-   Linear algebra costs B^ω = e^{ωμ₀} ≤ e^{2μ₀} when ω ≤ 2. This proves (a), and the ω-independence for fixed λ.
notes/suffmap_results.md-207-2. *(b).* Substitute λ = sF_{2α}, μ = bF_α. Then u = (s/b)x^α(log x)^{−α}, log u = (α + o(1))log x, and
notes/suffmap_results.md-208-   u log u = (αs/b)F_α. Minimising b + αs/b gives b = √(αs) and the value 2√(αs) = 2b.
notes/suffmap_results.md-209-   This is `optimum_fixed_size`; exact for the tested rationals.
notes/suffmap_results.md-210-3. *(c), lower bound.* The yield is Σ_{examined z} ρ(λ(z)/μ)^{1+o(1)} ≥ π(B)(1 − o(1)). Split the examined z into
notes/suffmap_results.md-211-   (1+ε)-adic size levels, with ε → 0 slowly. Some level λ carries a 1/L^{o(1)} share of the yield.
notes/suffmap_results.md-212-   - It contains ≤ e^{C_𝓕((1+ε)λ)} candidates.
