notes/suffmap_results.md-248-**Reading.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-249-- Exponent 1/3 is the line a/(2 − b) = 1/3. NFS realises (a, b) = (½, ½): candidate size grows like the square root
notes/suffmap_results.md-250-  of the entropy, times √x. (2/3, 0) is another point on the line: a fixed size x^{2/3}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-251-- Sub-1/3 needs either a smaller a, i.e. the candidate's size depends less on N, or a smaller b, i.e. it depends less on
notes/suffmap_results.md-252-  how many candidates there are.
notes/suffmap_results.md:253:- **α = 1/4, 1/5, 1/10 at b = 0 need laws λ ≍ x^{1/2}, x^{2/5}, x^{1/5}** (times logs): candidates whose size does not
notes/suffmap_results.md-254-  grow with the family. At b = ½ they need a = 3/8, 3/10, 3/20.
notes/suffmap_results.md-255-- Pigeonhole gives only λ(C) ≥ C − O(k log λ), when the candidates' smoothness tuples are distinct. That constrains
notes/suffmap_results.md-256-  nothing.
notes/suffmap_results.md-257-- For integer congruences u ≡ v (mod N), u ≠ v, with *both* sides random, max(|u|, |v|) ≥ N/2 (PROVED;
notes/suffmap_results.md-258-  `congruence_max_lower_bound`). So a = 1 and α ≥ 1/2. Below 1/2 one needs ring maps (NFS) or one side smooth by
notes/suffmap_results.md-259-  construction (QS: a square; listing: a product).
notes/suffmap_results.md-260-
notes/suffmap_results.md-261-### 1.3 Theorem LS (listing) [model-PROVED; pilist Table 1.1(A), smoothenum T2–T8 restated]
notes/suffmap_results.md-262-
notes/suffmap_results.md-263-A *lister* outputs candidates already known to be smooth on one side (one-sided) or on all sides (doubly). Cost is
notes/suffmap_results.md-264-charged per output, L[α, τ] each. Factor base L[α, κ].
notes/suffmap_results.md-265-
notes/suffmap_results.md-266-- **(a) Doubly.** The cost is L[α, max(κ + τ, 2κ)], for every α ∈ (0, 1), provided the region contains ≥ L[α, κ]
notes/suffmap_results.md-267-  relations and setup fits the bound. A fixed-α lister does **not** give polynomial time by shrinking B: L[α, τ] is
notes/suffmap_results.md-268-  superpolynomial for every fixed α > 0. Polynomial time needs a *separate* hypothesis (smoothenum T8): with
notes/suffmap_results.md-269-  y = (log N)^K, K > 1, and U = N^A, A > K/(K − 1), there must be polynomial setup and polynomial cost for every
notes/suffmap_results.md-270-  requested output prefix, including factorisations, duplicate removal and extraction.
notes/suffmap_results.md-271-- **(b) One-sided.** Suppose the residue side has log-size νF_{2α}. The cost is L[α, max(κ + τ + αν/κ, 2κ)].
notes/suffmap_results.md-272-  At τ = 0 this is Theorem SS(b) with s = ν: only the residue side counts.
notes/suffmap_results.md-273-- **(c) Existence fixes box sizes, not cost.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-274-  - Congruence instances need box area (U/N)·V ≥ L[1 − α]^{Θ(1)} (pilist §1.2). This reduces to U/N ≥ L[1 − α] when
notes/suffmap_results.md-275-    log V = o(F_{1−α}), i.e. V ≤ y, or V = L[2α] with α < 1/3. It does **not** reduce at α = 1/3 with V = L[2/3]
notes/suffmap_results.md:276:    (Prop SI), where U/N = L[1/3] suffices.
notes/suffmap_results.md-277-  - NFS-shaped doubly listing at factor base L[α] needs a box log-size x^γ with γ ≥ 1 − 2α.
notes/suffmap_results.md-278-  - In both cases the box is never paid.
notes/suffmap_results.md-279-- **(d) NFS-shaped partial listing.** Listing at E²·L[−θ·penalty] gives the curves c_rat(θ), c_alg(θ), c_both(θ) of
notes/suffmap_results.md-280-  smoothenum T2/T6/T7 and pilist Table 1.1(B).
notes/suffmap_results.md-281-
notes/suffmap_results.md-282-**Lemma SL (search ⇒ listing) [PROVED; `listing.list_by_search`].** Let O be a *complete* search oracle for a set S: on
notes/suffmap_results.md-283-[a, b] it returns some element of S ∩ [a, b], or correctly reports none. Then S ∩ [a, b] (K elements) can be listed with
notes/suffmap_results.md-284-≤ 2K + 1 calls. A union of J intervals needs ≤ 2K + J calls.
notes/suffmap_results.md-285-- *Proof.* After each hit z, recurse on [a, z − 1] and [z + 1, b], skipping empty intervals. Each element is returned
notes/suffmap_results.md-286-  exactly once, because the sub-intervals are disjoint and exclude z. Every call is either the root or one of the ≤ 2
notes/suffmap_results.md-287-  children of a hit, so there are ≤ 1 + 2K calls. ∎
notes/suffmap_results.md-288-- *Lexicographic version.* The same holds for any totally ordered region, e.g. pairs (k, v) in the Bohr set
notes/suffmap_results.md-289-  {kN + v : k ≤ K, |v| ≤ V}, provided the oracle accepts lexicographic intervals. Such an interval is ≤ 3 rectangles.
notes/suffmap_results.md-290-- *Sampler version.* A sampler returning near-uniform elements of S ∩ [a, b] lists a constant fraction in O(K) calls
notes/suffmap_results.md-291-  (coupon collector).
notes/suffmap_results.md-292-
notes/suffmap_results.md-293-**Prop PC (promise constructors) [PROVED, elementary; `AdversarialPromiseOracle`, `tiling_outputs`].** Let O be
notes/suffmap_results.md-294-guaranteed only on intervals of length ≥ h₀ (each such interval meets S). Let the range have length H, and let every gap
notes/suffmap_results.md-295-of S be ≤ h₀/4.
notes/suffmap_results.md-296-- (a) Against an adversarial choice of outputs, any algorithm obtains ≤ 4H/h₀ + 1 distinct elements.
notes/suffmap_results.md-297-  - The adversary's strategy: return an already-revealed element if the query contains one. Otherwise reveal the
notes/suffmap_results.md-298-    element of the query nearest the midpoint of the revealed-free gap containing it.
notes/suffmap_results.md-299-  - Each new point is then ≥ h₀/2 − h₀/4 from every earlier revealed point. If the gap midpoint lies inside the query,
notes/suffmap_results.md-300-    the new point is within one S-gap of the midpoint. If it lies outside, the new point is within one S-gap of the
notes/suffmap_results.md-301-    query end nearest the midpoint, and the query has length ≥ h₀.
notes/suffmap_results.md-302-- (b) Tiling by disjoint intervals of length h₀ achieves ≥ ⌊H/h₀⌋ distinct elements.
notes/suffmap_results.md-303-- So a promise constructor is worth **one output per h₀-tile**. Since the promise needs h₀ ≥ 4·(max gap), its value
notes/suffmap_results.md-304-  is governed by the ratio of h₀ to the mean gap 1/δ only under the HEURISTIC that max gap ≤ mean gap·L^{o(1)}
notes/suffmap_results.md-305-  (Poisson-like gaps; unproved for smooth numbers).
notes/suffmap_results.md-306-  - If h₀ = δ^{−1}·L^{o(1)}, it is as good as full listing at L-scale (θ = 1).
notes/suffmap_results.md-307-  - If h₀ = δ^{−1}·L[1/3, ηr], it *thins* the output set to one per tile (rational count penalty (1 + η)r), at no
notes/suffmap_results.md-308-    sieving cost. This is not the per-output-cost parameter θ of pilist.
notes/suffmap_results.md-309-  - In a doubly Bohr-set setting the region can be enlarged at polynomial cost, while ranges stay polynomially encoded
notes/suffmap_results.md-310-    (u ≤ N^A). There the useful number of tiles is what matters, not h₀/δ^{−1}. This requires a *blockwise* promise
notes/suffmap_results.md-311-    (every queried block of consecutive k contains a valid u), which does not follow from existence in the whole set
notes/suffmap_results.md-312-    (codex suffmap_2 #8).
notes/suffmap_results.md-313-  - Prop PC bounds distinct *oracle answers*. The θ readings are count-model substitutions: they need cheap calls and
notes/suffmap_results.md-314-    the same downstream randomness and fruitfulness assumptions as full listing.
notes/suffmap_results.md-315-- codex's blind statement "a constructor guaranteed only for sufficiently long intervals does not give that reduction"
notes/suffmap_results.md-316-  is correct when h₀ ≫ δ^{−1}. Prop PC quantifies it.
notes/suffmap_results.md-317-
notes/suffmap_results.md:318:### 1.4 Prop SI (single short intervals near multiples of N) [model-PROVED under H_S on the residue + R; `single_interval_program`]
notes/suffmap_results.md-319-
notes/suffmap_results.md-320-**Setting.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-321-- Factor base y = L[1/3, κ], intervals I_k = [kN, kN + h] with h = L[2/3, ν], k = 1, 2, ….
notes/suffmap_results.md-322-- An oracle constructs a y-smooth u_k ∈ I_k in L^{o(1)} time. The oracle's domain is the intervals I_k only. A promise
notes/suffmap_results.md-323-  constructor suffices, and complete search gives nothing more at L-scale (proof step 7).
notes/suffmap_results.md-324-- Each u_k gives the relation u_k ≡ v_k (mod N), v_k = u_k − kN ∈ [0, h].
notes/suffmap_results.md-325-
notes/suffmap_results.md-326-**Claim.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-327-- (i) The *mean* count of y-smooth integers per interval is L[2/3, ν − 2/(3κ)], which tends to infinity iff
notes/suffmap_results.md-328-  ν > 2/(3κ). Mean density is not uniform existence. For fixed N, y, h, existence for *every* k is false: CRT can place
notes/suffmap_results.md-329-  a large prime factor at every position of some interval. So the promise is restricted to the k ≤ L[1/3, C] actually
notes/suffmap_results.md-330-  used, and it is a hypothesis.
notes/suffmap_results.md-331-- For a constructor at fixed (κ, ν) with ν > 2/(3κ), the cost is L[1/3, max(κ + ν/(3κ), 2κ)]. The constant
notes/suffmap_results.md:332:  1.2114 + ε needs constructors with (κ, ν) near κ* = (2/9)^{1/3}, ν* = 2/(3κ*).
notes/suffmap_results.md-333-- (ii) The cost is L[1/3, max(κ + ν/(3κ), 2κ)]. Its infimum is approached as ν ↓ 2/(3κ) with κ³ = 2/9, giving
notes/suffmap_results.md:334:  **c = (16/9)^{1/3} + ε = 1.2114 + ε** for every ε > 0. This needs Heuristic R: fruitful, full-rank dependencies.
notes/suffmap_results.md-335-  A deterministic constructor whose output is a function of kN could make v non-random or the dependencies trivial,
notes/suffmap_results.md-336-  so the random-like-output assumption is explicit.
notes/suffmap_results.md-337-- (iii) With smoothness L[α], single intervals cost L[max(α, 1 − 2α)], so **α = 1/3 is optimal and exponent < 1/3 is
notes/suffmap_results.md-338-  impossible with single intervals**.
notes/suffmap_results.md-339-- (iv) *A separate, stronger hypothesis, not a consequence of (i)–(ii).* Take an oracle on Bohr-set unions
notes/suffmap_results.md-340-  ∪_{k∈[k₀, k₀+t₀]} [kN − V, kN + V] with V ≤ y, so the relation is doubly smooth automatically and the region can be
notes/suffmap_results.md-341-  enlarged for free (Prop PC). This is an instance of the doubly lister E3 and gives heuristic polynomial time, even
notes/suffmap_results.md-342-  with a promise constructor.
notes/suffmap_results.md-343-
notes/suffmap_results.md-344-**Proof.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-345-1. u ≍ N is y-smooth with probability L[2/3, −2/(3κ)]. This is CEP with u = x/(κF_{1/3}) and log u = (2/3)log x.
notes/suffmap_results.md-346-2. v ≤ h = L[2/3, ν] is y-smooth with probability L[1/3, −ν/(3κ)], by the same computation one level down. This is
notes/suffmap_results.md-347-   HEURISTIC: the oracle's choice must not bias v.
notes/suffmap_results.md-348-3. So L[1/3, κ] relations need L[1/3, κ + ν/(3κ)] calls, and linear algebra costs L[1/3, 2κ].
notes/suffmap_results.md-349-4. At ν = 2/(3κ) the trial term κ + 2/(9κ²) is decreasing for κ < (4/9)^{1/3} = 0.763. Its intersection with 2κ is at
notes/suffmap_results.md-350-   κ = (2/9)^{1/3} = 0.606 < 0.763, so the max is minimised there: c = 2κ = (16/9)^{1/3}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-351-5. (iii): an interval must have log-length ≥ (1 − α)F_{1−α}/κ to contain a smooth u. The residue then has log-size of
notes/suffmap_results.md-352-   order F_{1−α} and is smooth with probability L[1 − 2α]^{−Θ(1)}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-353-6. (iv): relations are B-smooth on both sides by construction. One oracle call per window of t₀ consecutive k gives one
notes/suffmap_results.md-354-   relation, whatever t₀ is. Existence needs smooth u ≤ N·k_max in residue windows of width 2V, i.e. equidistribution
notes/suffmap_results.md-355-   of very smooth numbers modulo N (HEURISTIC, far beyond the Harper/Drappeau range). Fruitfulness and rank need the
notes/suffmap_results.md-356-   oracle's outputs to be random-like (HEURISTIC). An adversarial oracle that knows p, q could return only useless
notes/suffmap_results.md-357-   relations.
notes/suffmap_results.md-358-7. *Complete search gives nothing more.*
notes/suffmap_results.md-359-   - Intervals below the threshold (ν < 2/(3κ)) are almost all empty, which wastes L[2/3, 2/(3κ) − ν] calls per hit.
notes/suffmap_results.md-360-   - Listing *all* hits in one interval leaves each output's residue penalty L[1/3, −ν/(3κ)] unchanged.
notes/suffmap_results.md-361-   - Searching for the smooth number nearest kN shrinks v below the gap g only with probability w/g per interval,
notes/suffmap_results.md-362-     which again costs an L[2/3] factor. ∎
notes/suffmap_results.md-363-
notes/suffmap_results.md-364-**Robustness to oracle cost.** If each oracle call costs L[1/3, τ], the trial term becomes κ + τ + 2/(9κ²). At ω = 2
notes/suffmap_results.md-365-this beats MNFS 1.9019 iff τ < 1.9019 − (3/2)(4/9)^{1/3} = 0.757 (at κ = (4/9)^{1/3} = 0.763; proof critic m4(d)).
notes/suffmap_results.md-366-So the comparison with MNFS does not depend on the oracle being exactly L^{o(1)}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-367-
notes/suffmap_results.md-368-**Remarks.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-369-- The constant (16/9)^{1/3} coincides with Kirchner's special-n constant and with resbarrier's k = 1 rank-2 floor. The
notes/suffmap_results.md-370-  arithmetic reason: the relation's only random side has log-size (2/(3κ))F_{2/3}, which is half the base-m norm ledger.
notes/suffmap_results.md:371:- Prop SI is QS with the square replaced by a smooth number. Squares near kN have gaps ≍ √N, which forces v ≍ √N and
notes/suffmap_results.md-372-  L[1/2]. L[1/3]-smooth numbers have gaps L[2/3], which gives v ≍ L[2/3] and L[1/3].
notes/suffmap_results.md:373:- Codex's blind round derived the same optimum independently: b = 0.6057, s = 1.1006, c = 1.2114 (§6.1).
notes/suffmap_results.md-374-- Relation to Schnorr: u = ∏p^{e_p} ∈ I_k is a sparse inhomogeneous approximation Σe_p log p ∈ [log kN, log(kN + h)],
notes/suffmap_results.md-375-  with ‖e‖₁ ≤ x/log 2. That is a one-sided form of Schnorr's CVP target (schnorrcvp).
notes/suffmap_results.md-376-- smoothenum Prop W shows that balanced, height-bounded k-trees cannot do it (k ≤ 2, meet-in-the-middle, exponential).
notes/suffmap_results.md-377-  The pilist Johnson-radius class certifies one point per call in this congruence scaling, which is outside its NFS
notes/suffmap_results.md-378-  scope.
