notes/suffmap_results.md-426-**E2. One-sided Bohr-set lister.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-427-- *Statement.* Lists y-smooth u ≤ U, y = L[α, κ], with |u mod N| ≤ L[2α, ν], at L[α, τ] per output, τ + 2√(αν) < c.
notes/suffmap_results.md-428-- *Gives* L[α, c].
notes/suffmap_results.md-429-- *Extra:* H on the residue, R, and modulus-generic random-like outputs.
notes/suffmap_results.md-430-- *Relation.*
notes/suffmap_results.md-431-  - ⇔ Π-nonexact one-sided (pilist).
notes/suffmap_results.md-432-  - Contains the dense-PTE / systematic-equation escapes (systeq §6, ptesplit O1, ptecons). Their exact versions are
notes/suffmap_results.md-433-    refuted (ptebarrier Theorem W).
notes/suffmap_results.md-434-  - Smooth numbers in Bohr sets; one-sided Schnorr CVP.
notes/suffmap_results.md-435-  - ⇒ Prop DLT (below).
notes/suffmap_results.md-436-- *Believed* false: the box sieve costs L[1 − α] per output.
notes/suffmap_results.md-437-- *Implication:* model-PROVED.
notes/suffmap_results.md-438-
notes/suffmap_results.md-439-**E3. Doubly-smooth lister.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-440-- *Statement.* Lists u ≡ v (mod N), both y-smooth (or both NFS norms smooth), at L[α, τ] per output.
notes/suffmap_results.md-441-- *Gives* any α (Theorem LS(a)), and heuristic polynomial time.
notes/suffmap_results.md-442-- *Extra:* R; existence (equidistribution of very smooth numbers mod N, HEURISTIC; or, unconditionally for some
notes/suffmap_results.md-443-  catalogues, nonexact Prop AB).
notes/suffmap_results.md-444-- *Equivalent forms* (PROVED, elementary):
notes/suffmap_results.md-445-  - (i) S-unit pairs u − v = kN with u, v ∈ Γ_S (abcsunit Prop SU). abcsunit Lemma FR sharpens the fruitfulness
notes/suffmap_results.md-446-    requirement: the relations must generate Λ_N up to odd index; near-full parity rank is not enough. abcsunit Prop EX
notes/suffmap_results.md-447-    proves existence (abundance) unconditionally at y = (log N)^κ. This is a correspondence of *witnesses*, not of
notes/suffmap_results.md-448-    algorithms:
notes/suffmap_results.md-449-    standard fixed-coefficient S-unit solvers do not enumerate over the free multiplier k at the required cost;
notes/suffmap_results.md-450-  - (ii) nonzero vectors of the relation lattice Λ_N = {e ∈ Z^{π(B)} : ∏p^{e_p} ≡ 1 (mod N)}, of polynomial ℓ₁-norm
notes/suffmap_results.md-451-    (Schnorr 1991; Adleman 1995). Any vector of Λ_N is a doubly-smooth relation, whatever its length. But the target is
notes/suffmap_results.md-452-    a *fruitful, full-rank* set of vectors of polynomial ℓ₁-norm: φ(N)·e_i are in Λ_N and useless. Approximate CVP in Schnorr's lattice is a *method* for producing them, not the target
notes/suffmap_results.md-453-    itself;
notes/suffmap_results.md-454-  - Precedent for (ii): Stange, arXiv 2211.06821, Thm 3.2. Random small multiplicative relations mod n over a factor
notes/suffmap_results.md-455-    base give the order, hence a factor (Monte Carlo, poly(b, log n)).
notes/suffmap_results.md-456-  - (iii) constructive navigation in Cay((Z/N)^*, {p ≤ B}). Words equal to 1 are E3 directly. The stronger *target*
notes/suffmap_results.md-457-    version is **PROVED sufficient with no heuristic** (codex suffmap_1 §3.3).
notes/suffmap_results.md-458-    - *Hypothesis:* known primes p ≤ B generate (Z/N)^*, and any target can be expressed as a word with
notes/suffmap_results.md-459-      polynomial-bit exponents in polynomial time.
notes/suffmap_results.md-460-    - *Proof:* navigate a_i² for hidden uniform units a_i and take a parity dependency among π(B) + 1 exponent
notes/suffmap_results.md-461-      vectors. This gives b with b² = ∏a_i². The hidden ∏a_i is uniform among the square roots of ∏a_i², so
notes/suffmap_results.md-462-      gcd(b − ∏a_i, N) splits N with probability ≥ 1/2.
notes/suffmap_results.md-463-    - *Conclusion:* randomized polynomial-time factoring. Navigation to arbitrary targets is DL mod N relative to the
notes/suffmap_results.md-464-      factor base, so this is KNOWN in substance (Bach 1984) and belongs with the one-way oracles of E7.
notes/suffmap_results.md-465-  - ⇒ Prop DLT.
notes/suffmap_results.md-466-- *Believed* false.
notes/suffmap_results.md-467-  - Generic lower bounds forbid computing orders or relations in black-box groups of unknown order cheaply (Babai 1999;
notes/suffmap_results.md-468-    Sutherland, MIT thesis 2007, §2.2: Ω(N^{1/3}) generic order computation; Damgård–Koprowski 2002 for root
notes/suffmap_results.md-469-    extraction; Shoup 1997 is for known order). So any method must use integer structure.
notes/suffmap_results.md-470-  - The only known integer structure is sieving.
notes/suffmap_results.md-471-- *Implication:* model-PROVED (smoothenum T7/T8).
notes/suffmap_results.md-472-
notes/suffmap_results.md-473-**Prop DLT (listing transfers to prime-field DL) [PROVED reduction, modulo the standard index-calculus heuristics].**
notes/suffmap_results.md-474-- *Statement.* Let a lister for E2 or E3 use the modulus M only through arithmetic mod M (modulus-generic). Suppose it
notes/suffmap_results.md-475-  also handles cosets u ≡ t·v (mod M) for a given t.
notes/suffmap_results.md-476-- *Consequence.* Running it with M = p prime collects factor-base relations and individual-log relations for F_p^* at
notes/suffmap_results.md-477-  the same L-*exponent*, which gives L_p[α] discrete logarithms. The constant is not claimed: it depends on the linear
notes/suffmap_results.md-478-  algebra over Z/(p−1), the rank modulo every prime power ℓ^e ∥ p − 1, generator normalisation, and the cost of
notes/suffmap_results.md-479-  factoring p − 1 (codex suffmap_2 #2). Without the coset capability the individual-log step is not covered.
notes/suffmap_results.md-480-- *Proof.* Relations ∏p_i^{e_i} ≡ t^{ε}∏p_j^{f_j} (mod p) are linear equations in the logs over Z/(p−1). The linear
notes/suffmap_results.md-481-  algebra is polynomial in the factor-base size, which preserves α. Rank mod each ℓ | p − 1 is the usual heuristic. ∎
notes/suffmap_results.md-482-- *Reading.* Prime-field DL has been stuck at 1.902 (Matyukhin 2003; Commeine–Semaev 2006) (heurnfs §5(ii)). So any
notes/suffmap_results.md-483-  modulus-generic E2/E3 lister would break two problems believed hard. This parallels prepread's Corollary O1-N. A
notes/suffmap_results.md-484-  lister that exploits compositeness escapes Prop DLT, but no such mechanism is known.
notes/suffmap_results.md-485-
notes/suffmap_results.md-486-**E4. Short straight-line programs for Pochhammer polynomials.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-487-- *Statement.* For n = 2^j ≤ √N, the polynomials P_n(X) = 2^{e(n)}∏_{a=1}^{n}(X − a) have constant-free SLPs of size
notes/suffmap_results.md-488-  (log n)^{O(1)}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-489-- *Gives* factoring in **P/poly**, rigorously and with no heuristic.
notes/suffmap_results.md-490-  - Store SLPs for P_{2^j}, j ≤ log₂√N, as poly(log N) advice.
notes/suffmap_results.md-491-  - Compute k! mod N for any k from the binary blocks, as ∏_j P_{2^j}(s_j) up to signs and powers of 2, which are units
notes/suffmap_results.md-492-    mod odd N.
notes/suffmap_results.md-493-  - Binary search on k with gcd(k! mod N, N).
notes/suffmap_results.md-494-  - If the SLPs are constructible in polynomial time from n, factoring is in P.
notes/suffmap_results.md-495-  - A *uniform* variant needs no SLPs at all: computing k! mod N in time poly(log N + log k) gives deterministic
notes/suffmap_results.md-496-    polynomial-time factoring by binary search (codex suffmap_1 §2.8). The integer-SLP question (τ) and the uniform
notes/suffmap_results.md-497-    modular question are distinct.
notes/suffmap_results.md-498-  - The weakest known sufficient statement for N-oblivious SLP methods is slpcirc's root-density condition W_U(s, μ)
notes/suffmap_results.md-499-    (slpcirc §1.3). It is necessary up to a factor 2 within that model, and it generalises E4.
notes/suffmap_results.md-500-- *Relation.*
notes/suffmap_results.md-501-  - ⇐ the permanent has polynomial-size constant-free circuits. That is Bürgisser 2009 (Comput. Complexity 18,
notes/suffmap_results.md-502-    81–103; ECCC TR06-113 Theorem 1.1, stated contrapositively; the hypothesis τ(Per_n) = n^{O(1)} is constant-free
notes/suffmap_results.md-503-    *and division-free*). The Pochhammer form is Thm 4.1(2): τ(2^{e(n)}∏(X − k)) = (log n)^{O(1)}, division-free and
notes/suffmap_results.md-504-    constant-free. "Allowing divisions" in the abstract refers only to the Taylor polynomials (Thm 4.1(3)). So the
notes/suffmap_results.md-505-    division-free requirement of E4 is met; this was checked by the literature critic in the ECCC text, with the journal
notes/suffmap_results.md-506-    numbering not checked. The n! form
notes/suffmap_results.md-507-    (τ(n!) polylog for all n) needs the uniformity or advice caveat below. The permanent hypothesis implies VP = VNP.
notes/suffmap_results.md-508-  - Contradicted by the Shub–Smale τ-conjecture. P_n has n integer roots, so the conjecture forces τ(P_n) ≥ n^{Ω(1)}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-509-  - Strassen 1976 and Lipton 1994 for the factoring link.
notes/suffmap_results.md-510-- *Believed* false, on the evidence of the τ-conjecture. VP ≠ VNP is *not* evidence: the implication runs Per easy ⇒
notes/suffmap_results.md-511-  Pochhammer easy, and VP ≠ VNP is consistent with short Pochhammer SLPs.
notes/suffmap_results.md-512-- *Implication:* KNOWN (folklore, slpcirc Prop BB).
notes/suffmap_results.md-513-- **Landmark either way:** a hardness proof would be a constant-free permanent lower bound (Bürgisser), and an easiness
notes/suffmap_results.md-514-  proof would be nonuniform polynomial-time factoring.
notes/suffmap_results.md-515-
notes/suffmap_results.md-516-**E5. Complexity collapses.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-517-- *Statements and consequences.*
notes/suffmap_results.md-518-  - "N has a factor ≤ k" is in UP ∩ coUP (sorted factorisation + AKS certificate; polyimpl). So P = UP ∩ coUP, or
notes/suffmap_results.md-519-    P = NP ∩ coNP, puts factoring in P.
notes/suffmap_results.md-520-  - BQP ⊆ BPP puts it in BPP (Shor 1997). It is enough to simulate classically the specific order-finding circuits of
notes/suffmap_results.md-521-    Shor's algorithm, which is weaker than BQP ⊆ BPP. Classical order finding for a ↦ a^x mod N is itself
notes/suffmap_results.md-522-    randomized-equivalent to factoring (Miller 1976).
notes/suffmap_results.md-523-  - Jeřábek 2016 (JCSS 82, Thm 3.7) gives randomized reductions of factoring to PPA and to PWPP; Thm 3.8
notes/suffmap_results.md-524-    derandomises them under GRH_q (GRH for quadratic characters). So polynomial algorithms for PPA or PWPP give randomized polynomial-time factoring.
notes/suffmap_results.md-525-- *Believed* false. *Implication:* KNOWN, trivial. No structure.
notes/suffmap_results.md-526-
notes/suffmap_results.md-527-**E6. Group-order families with biased smoothness.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-528-- *Statement (corrected after codex suffmap_2 #12).* An efficiently represented, efficiently samplable family of
notes/suffmap_results.md-529-  groups and points over Z/N, with L_p[α, β]-cost annihilation stages. For each p | N, with probability
--
notes/suffmap_results.md-564-  average.
notes/suffmap_results.md-565-All are believed false and give no structure; they belong with E5.
notes/suffmap_results.md-566-
notes/suffmap_results.md-567-### Tier C: an L[1/3] constant only
notes/suffmap_results.md-568-
notes/suffmap_results.md:569:**C1. Prop SI (single intervals near kN).**
notes/suffmap_results.md:570:- *Gives* 1.2114.
notes/suffmap_results.md:571:- *Statement and assumptions:* §1.4. The existence half is "every interval [X, X + L_X[2/3, ν]] contains an
notes/suffmap_results.md-572-  L_X[1/3, κ]-smooth integer for ν > 2/(3κ)". That is a smooth-numbers-in-all-short-intervals statement at a scale far
notes/suffmap_results.md-573-  beyond current results. All-interval results: Hildebrand 1986; Friedlander–Granville 1993 (y ≥ exp((log x)^{5/6+o(1)}));
notes/suffmap_results.md-574-  Croot 2007; Matomäki 2016. Almost-all results: Hildebrand–Tenenbaum 1993; Jain, arXiv 2502.10530
notes/suffmap_results.md-575-  (y ≥ exp((log X)^{2/3+ε})). Nothing reaches y = L[1/3].
notes/suffmap_results.md-576-- *Recognised neighbour:* deterministic construction of smooth numbers in a given interval. Boneh (STOC 2000 / JCSS 64
notes/suffmap_results.md-577-  (2002), Thm 3.1) works only in the polylog-y regime, log x < y < (log² x)/5 per Granville 2008 §5.7, on windows of
notes/suffmap_results.md:578:  length x^{(log x)/(4y)}. At Prop SI's scale those windows are shorter than 1, so the method is vacuous there.
notes/suffmap_results.md-579-  Boneh's §4 sketches a QS use, states no reduction and no constant, and notes the bounds are insufficient for QS.
notes/suffmap_results.md:580:- *Novelty (literature critic).* No prior statement of the reduction or of 1.2114 was found. It is, however, an
notes/suffmap_results.md-581-  immediate instance of Pomerance's principle with X = h = L[2/3] under the constraint that intervals must contain
notes/suffmap_results.md-582-  smooth numbers. Its closest structural analogues are Schroeppel's linear sieve and the cubic sieve
notes/suffmap_results.md-583-  (Coppersmith–Odlyzko–Schroeppel 1986), where one side is constructed and the residue is N^{1/2} or N^{1/3}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-584-- *Believed:* open; no method below exhaustive sieving per output is known. Prop W and the pilist Johnson-radius class
notes/suffmap_results.md-585-  fail here.
notes/suffmap_results.md-586-- *Implication:* model-PROVED. No prior statement found by us.
notes/suffmap_results.md-587-
notes/suffmap_results.md-588-**C2. Complete search or sampler for smooth values on NFS rational rows.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-589-- *Parameters:* X = L[2/3], h = L[1/3], y = L[1/3]; in X-units h, y = L_X[1/2].
notes/suffmap_results.md-590-- *Gives* 1.6386 (smoothenum T3, KNOWN as the factory per-N program), via Lemma SL. A promise constructor gives the same
notes/suffmap_results.md-591-  if h₀ = gap·L^{o(1)} (Prop PC). A partial version gives c(θ) with slope −0.21.
notes/suffmap_results.md-592-- *Dominated by C1 in constant*, but it has different parameters.
notes/suffmap_results.md-593-- Practical precedent: Hittmeir, "Smooth Subsum Search" (arXiv 2301.10529), which constructs candidates divisible by
notes/suffmap_results.md-594-  many factor-base primes; a constant-factor heuristic.
notes/suffmap_results.md-595-
notes/suffmap_results.md-596-**C3. Algebraic-side rows (smooth values of F(·, b)).**
notes/suffmap_results.md-597-- *Gives* inf 0.7631 (smoothenum T6; 0.8246 at the e ≤ 3 cap).
notes/suffmap_results.md-598-- Doubly row search gives inf 0 at L[1/3]. Growing boxes then give E3.
notes/suffmap_results.md-599-- **Caveat (codex suffmap_2 #3).** Row-by-row search pays the 2K + J startup term: E·V row calls, i.e. L[1/3, e + v].
notes/suffmap_results.md:600:  The T6 infimum is approached with e → ∞, so a row-wise implementation does not reach it. Codex reports 1.16477 for
notes/suffmap_results.md-601-  the program with the e + v term added; we have not checked this. Reaching 0.7631 or 0 needs a whole-box
notes/suffmap_results.md-602-  two-dimensional oracle that can skip empty rows. C2 is unaffected, because E ≪ E²L[−r] at its optimum.
notes/suffmap_results.md-603-
notes/suffmap_results.md-604-**C4. Beyond-Johnson, output-sensitive CRT list decoding of the factor-base code; H_sel violations.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-605-- *Gives* c(θ′) via pilist Remark SEL (c). Within the Johnson radius the gain is L^{o(1)} (Prop JR).
notes/suffmap_results.md-606-- *Relation:* CRT list decoding (Goldreich–Ron–Sudan; Boneh; Guruswami–Sahai–Sudan). Plain-RS capacity decoding is a
notes/suffmap_results.md-607-  2026 preprint (Brakensiek et al.), with no CRT analogue at vanishing rate.
notes/suffmap_results.md-608-- *Believed* implausible: pilist puts it below 1%.
notes/suffmap_results.md-609-
notes/suffmap_results.md-610-**C5. Sparse F₂ linear algebra in K^{ω+o(1)}, ω < 2, for K^{1+o(1)} nonzeros.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-611-- *Gives* MNFS 1.8595 at ω ≲ 1.67 (heurnfs B4). By Theorem SS(c) no single-family method moves.
notes/suffmap_results.md-612-- *Relation:* "solve sparse linear systems over finite fields faster than Wiedemann's n·nnz" (Wiedemann 1986; block
notes/suffmap_results.md-613-  Wiedemann, Coppersmith 1994; Eberly–Giesbrecht–Giorgi–Storjohann–Villard 2006/07). Peng–Vempala (SODA 2021) give
notes/suffmap_results.md-614-  O(n^{2.33}) for poly-conditioned real systems with O(n) nonzeros. That beats matrix multiplication but is *worse*
notes/suffmap_results.md-615-  than n·nnz ≈ n², so it says nothing about ω < 2.
notes/suffmap_results.md-616-- *Believed* open; the least implausible item on the map.
notes/suffmap_results.md-617-
notes/suffmap_results.md-618-**C6. Small-height polynomial pairs for general N.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-619-- *Statement:* heights with κ < 2 in heurnfs's ledger.
notes/suffmap_results.md-620-- *Gives* (32κ/9)^{1/3}, never an exponent. resbarrier C2/RB2 gives α ≥ 1/3 for every rank-2 pair and every N *within
notes/suffmap_results.md-621-  its model*: §2.2-form extraction, D ≥ 3, box or lattice side ≤ B^{1−o(1)} (H5), heuristic H, B² linear algebra, single
notes/suffmap_results.md-622-  N.
notes/suffmap_results.md-623-- *Status:* **PROVED false for almost all N** at the height level (resbarrier GAE). The discriminant version is open.
notes/suffmap_results.md-624-- **Small-discriminant number fields with a root mod N** are this item. N² | disc is forced for two-map extraction and
notes/suffmap_results.md-625-  N | disc for ramified extraction (Lemma ND). Bounded root discriminant (Golod–Shafarevich towers) gives small-norm
notes/suffmap_results.md-626-  ideals but no linked rank-2 family.
notes/suffmap_results.md-627-- Small discriminant plus a ring map is not even a size certificate on its own (codex). Q has discriminant 1, and its
notes/suffmap_results.md-628-  ring map Z → Z/N gives candidates a − bm as large as m. What matters is the height or discriminant of the *linked
notes/suffmap_results.md-629-  pair*.
notes/suffmap_results.md-630-
notes/suffmap_results.md-631-**C7. Kirchner 2018 fields.**
notes/suffmap_results.md:632:- *Gives* 1.2114 for very special n (k = 1). Excluded by GAE-ram for almost all N.
notes/suffmap_results.md-633-- The relaxed version gives 1.5263 "for most n". It is not excluded by GAE-ram, but needs a Galois-subfield structure and
notes/suffmap_results.md-634-  a generator that nobody has.
notes/suffmap_results.md-635-- Kirchner's abstract: (16/9)^{1/3} for special n; for general numbers it "may reach" (32/9)^{1/3} "if the method can
notes/suffmap_results.md-636-  be adapted"; "there is no evidence it can be done". The full-text phrases quoted in resbarrier §4.1 were not
notes/suffmap_results.md-637-  re-checked by our literature critic.
notes/suffmap_results.md-638-- *Implication:* conditional claim, not verified by us (resbarrier §4.1).
notes/suffmap_results.md-639-
notes/suffmap_results.md-640-**C8. Non-uniform precomputation (the factorization factory).** 1.6386 per N after L[1/3, 2.0069], KNOWN (Coppersmith
notes/suffmap_results.md-641-1993; Bernstein–Lange 2014). Not single-N RAM.
notes/suffmap_results.md-642-
notes/suffmap_results.md-643-### Tier X: the exponential regime
notes/suffmap_results.md-644-
notes/suffmap_results.md-645-**X1. Coppersmith / partial key exposure.**
notes/suffmap_results.md-646-- *Statement:* poly-time factoring from a fraction f < 1/2 of p's top bits.
notes/suffmap_results.md-647-- *Gives* N^{f/2}. It beats the deterministic N^{1/5} record (Harvey, Math. Comp. 90, 2021; log-log speedup by
notes/suffmap_results.md-648-  Harvey–Hittmeir, Math. Comp. 91, 2022) iff f < 2/5. The topblock ladder needs f = 2/(j+2).
notes/suffmap_results.md-649-- For L[α] one would need f·x/2 = O(F_α). That is factoring itself up to guessing: circular (topblock).
notes/suffmap_results.md-650-
notes/suffmap_results.md-651-**X2. Coverage.** dchworst Theorem R: c > 3/8 gives a worst case below N^{1/5}; DCH_pair gives N^{1/6}.
notes/suffmap_results.md-652-
notes/suffmap_results.md-653-**X3. Polylog evaluation of degree-2 floor sums.** nonsmooth §6, floorsum: exponential-regime ladders only.
