notes/suffmap_results.md-709-
notes/suffmap_results.md:710:**Most worth attacking: C1 (Prop SI).**
notes/suffmap_results.md-711-- It is a single, clean question: construct L[1/3]-smooth numbers in intervals of length L[2/3] near multiples of N.
notes/suffmap_results.md-712-- It sits at an untested scale (between Boneh's CRT decoding and Schnorr's CVP).
notes/suffmap_results.md-713-- It has no barrier beyond method classes (Prop W, LQ, JR in other scalings).
notes/suffmap_results.md-714-- Its payoff would be the largest known constant drop, 1.90 → 1.21.
notes/suffmap_results.md-715-- A natural first test: is it equivalent to the NFS-scale smooth-interval problem C2, or to one-sided Schnorr CVP at
notes/suffmap_results.md-716-  dimension L[1/3]?
notes/suffmap_results.md-717-- E4 and E5 score higher on the raw product, but they are not attackable research targets in this project.
notes/suffmap_results.md-718-- This is an assessment. The payoff rests on extra oracle-distribution assumptions (random residues, fruitfulness).
notes/suffmap_results.md-719-  Codex's adversarial round prefers C2 and C5 for their clearer interfaces (§6.2).
notes/suffmap_results.md-720-
notes/suffmap_results.md-721-**Landmark either way: E4.** Both outcomes are major theorems: nonuniform polynomial-time factoring, or a constant-free
notes/suffmap_results.md-722-permanent lower bound. The hardness must be quantified over the allowed power-of-two multiples.
notes/suffmap_results.md-723-- This is not literally unique. Any unconditional superpolynomial lower bound for an NP search problem, e.g. for the
notes/suffmap_results.md:724:  Prop SI constructor, would imply P ≠ NP (codex suffmap_2 #17).
notes/suffmap_results.md-725-- E4 is distinguished because its negative side is a natural, actively studied lower-bound problem (Bürgisser's
notes/suffmap_results.md-726-  route to VP⁰ ≠ VNP⁰), rather than P ≠ NP itself.
notes/suffmap_results.md-727-
notes/suffmap_results.md-728-**Highest-profile exponent target: E3**, together with its high-profile faces: S-unit enumeration, Schnorr's lattice,
notes/suffmap_results.md-729-Cayley-graph navigation. Prop DLT makes it implausible.
