notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-296-### 2.6 Derivation [MODEL; checked by the proof critic and codex]
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-297-
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-298-- ln B = bL^α(ln L)^{1−α}, and u ln u = sL^α(ln L)^{1−α}. So u = (s/α)L^α(ln L)^{−α} and
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-299-  θ = (sb/α)L^{2α−1}(ln L)^{1−2α}.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-300-- With unit-cost calls, the calls cost L[α, b + s] and linear algebra L[α, 2b]. The optimum s = b gives L[α, 2b], so
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-301-  θ = τ(…) gives L[α, 2√(ατ)].
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-302-- With cost n^c, the calls cost L[α, (1+c)b + s], giving 2√((1+c)ατ).
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-303-- α = 1/2: θ = (γ²−1)/2 gives L[1/2, √(γ²−1)].
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-304-- α = 1/3: beating GNFS 1.923 needs τ < 2.77/(1+c).
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-305-
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-306-### 2.7 Prop NP [PROVED from known theorems; folklore]
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-307-
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-308-- **CVP.** {(N, k) : N has a divisor in [2, k]} ∈ NP. GapCVP_γ is NP-hard for γ = n^{c/lglg n} under deterministic
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-309-  Karp reductions (Dinur–Kindler–Raz–Safra, Combinatorica 23 (2003)). Adaptive binary search therefore gives a
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-310-  deterministic polynomial-time *Turing* reduction of the factoring function to GapCVP_γ on lattices of dimension
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-311-  poly(log N). The queries satisfy the gap promise.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-312-- **SVP.** GapSVP_γ is NP-hard under randomized reductions for every constant γ (Ajtai 1998; Micciancio 2001;
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-313-  Khot 2005; Haviv–Regev 2007/ToC 2012 Thm 3.1). So factoring ≤_R GapSVP_γ.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-314-- **Folklore, not ours.** The SchnorrGate README says Ajtai "instead found a proof of a much stronger statement:
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-315-  namely that SVP is NP-hard", while trying to complete Adleman's goal. Micciancio (2012 talk) says the prime lattice
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-316-  "played a fundamental role in establishing the NP-hardness of SVP". Ducas's RISC slides p. 22 say the same.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-317-  - Adleman's own framing is in §1 of his draft: "the first substantial evidence that Lattice is not in P".
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-318-- **Newer claims (not refereed, not checked by us).**
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-319-  - "Ten Advances in Mathematics and Theoretical Computer Science" (OpenAI, updated 6 Aug 2026), Ch. 7, claims a
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md:320:    deterministic Karp reduction 3SAT → GapCVP^{(2)}_{n^{1/400}}. If correct, Prop NP extends to γ ≤ n^{1/400}.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-321-  - Hair–Sahai (ePrint 2025/2181) claim deterministic SVP hardness in ℓ_p, p > 2. That does not cover ℓ2.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-322-- **Upper side.** GapCVP_γ ∈ NP ∩ coAM for γ = O(√(n/log n)) (Goldreich–Goldwasser 2000), and ∈ NP ∩ coNP for
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-323-  γ = C√n (Aharonov–Regev 2005, Thm 1.1). LWE-based cryptography uses γ = Õ(n)–poly(n) (Regev, STOC 2005 /
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-324-  JACM 56(6) 2009).
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-325-- **Consequences.**
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-326-  - (i) Theorem H's γ = 1 + n^{−ε} lies inside the NP-hard range. Its content is the restriction to an explicit
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-327-    family, which is incomparable with the general-lattice reduction.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-328-  - (ii) No reduction from factoring to GapCVP_γ is known for γ ≥ √(n/log n). Such a reduction would contradict
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-329-    nothing, since factoring ∈ NP ∩ coNP. This route's certificate cannot supply one; whether any route can is open.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-330-  - (iii) Conversely, faster factoring is not known to help any lattice problem. Prime-lattice CVP asks for smooth u
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-331-    near smooth multiples of N, and N's factors do not help. Ducas–Pierrot (ePrint 2018/146) decode discrete-log
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-332-    lattices *by* factoring small integers; that is a different family and the trivial direction.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-333-  - (iv) A poly-time CVP_{1+n^{−ε}} algorithm on prime lattices alone would heuristically factor. We know of no
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-334-    evidence that CVP on this family is easier than worst-case CVP.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-335-
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-336-## 3. Counting relations [HEURISTIC H_S; MODEL]
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-337-
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-338-**Shell estimate** (codex: these are *shell* exponents, not cumulative counts).
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-339-- For B = (ln N)^κ, Ψ(X, B) = X^{1−1/κ+o(1)}.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-340-- The expected number of relations in the shell v ≍ N^a, |w| ≍ N^b (u ≍ vN, fixed a, b) is
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-341-  N^{e(a,b)+o(1)} with e(a, b) = a(1−2/κ) + b(1−1/κ) − 1/κ.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-342-- Close-vector relations have b < 1 + a. Maximising over admissible shells:
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-343-
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-344-| κ | max shell exponent over b ≤ 1 + a | small residual (b → 0) |
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-345-|---|---|---|
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-346-| κ < 3/2 | negative for every shell (e(a, 1+a) = a(2 − 3/κ) + 1 − 2/κ < 0) | negative |
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-347-| 3/2 < κ < 2 | positive only for a > (2−κ)/(2κ−3) | negative |
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-348-| κ > 2 | positive already at a = 0 (b > 1/(κ−1)) | positive for a > 1/(κ−2) (Theorem H) |
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-349-
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-350-- These are heuristic shell thresholds, not proofs of non-existence. The boundary cases and the tail over
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-351-  unbounded heights are not treated asymptotically. For fixed B, Ψ grows only polylogarithmically, so the shell
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-352-  contributions decay; E0 checks the tail numerically.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-353-- With multiplier targets (v free, Adleman's form), shells are positive for every κ > 1, but need
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-354-  N^{(2−κ)/(2(κ−1))} targets.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-355-- For 3/2 < κ < 2, the closest vector at moderate scale C has |w| ≈ N^{1/κ}, smooth with probability N^{−1/κ²}.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-356-
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-357-**Finite sizes (E0) [MODEL, naive H_S].**
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-358-- Primitive and non-primitive pairs (v, w) are summed alike. ln v is truncated at 1.5L + 50, and widening it to 3L
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-359-  changed log₁₀E_tot by 0.000.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-360-- Densities are midpoints of the proved cumulative brackets. Codex notes that the *density* lower brackets are 0 at
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-361-  these sizes, so the densities are model values, not certified. The cumulative counts are tight, e.g.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-362-  10^{62.20} ≤ Ψ(2^{800}, 499) ≤ 10^{62.35}.
