notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-456-6. **Other literature on the required dimension.**
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-457-   - Grebnev et al. (IEEE Access 11 (2023), arXiv 2303.04656) state that no estimate of the required n as a function
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-458-     of N had been derived "neither theoretically, nor empirically". §3/E0 supplies a model estimate.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-459-   - Empirical scaling, all finding exponential behaviour:
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-460-     - Aboumrad–Widdows–Kaushik (arXiv 2308.07804, Figs 1, 3, Table 1);
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-461-     - Sato–Auzemery–Katayama–Yasuda (IWSEC 2024);
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-462-     - Khattar–Yosri (arXiv 2307.09651; ≤ 70 bits);
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-463-     - Ritter–Rössner (ePrint 1997/008; 60 bits).
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-464-   - Tesoro et al. (Phys. Rev. A 113, 032418 (2026)) claim numerical polynomial scaling up to 100 bits. Their
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-465-     Yan-type variant tests smoothness of w over an enlarged base (B₂ ≈ n² primes), which changes the count. Our model
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-466-     with B₂ ≫ B would raise E_tot, and has not been run. It is not a contradiction at ≤ 100 bits, where E0 already
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-467-     shows modest n suffices.
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-468-   - Ducas (RISC slide 21): "A solid average-case complexity analysis is still missing". §3 is a step toward it, not a
notes/schnorrcvp_results.md-469-     proof.
