    30	**Plain language.**
    31	- We found **no sufficient condition that is both plausible and gives an exponent below 1/3.** Every sufficient
    32	  condition on the map is one of three kinds:
    33	  1. **as strong as polynomial-time factoring in disguise**, and usually also a breakthrough elsewhere (prime-field
    34	     discrete logarithms, algebraic circuit complexity, quantum simulation);
    35	  2. **only an L[1/3] constant**, some of them tied to concrete, attackable problems;
    36	  3. **already refuted for typical N**, or a model in which the target is absent under the standard counting
    37	     heuristics.
    38	- **The organising theorem is the size–entropy law (Theorem SS).** For a method that *samples* candidates and waits for
    39	  them to be smooth over *one shared factor base*, with every examined candidate tested in full (model S, §1.1),
    40	  everything is decided by one function λ(C): the smallest total log-size of the smoothness integers when e^C
    41	  candidates are examined. The cost is e^{C*}, where C* is the least C with C² ≥ 2λ(C)·log λ(C). For a power
    42	  law λ = A·x^a(log x)^{1−a−b}C^b this gives **α = a/(2 − b)** and **c^{2−b} = 4αA**. The formula reproduces Dixon √2,
    43	  QS 1, GNFS (64/9)^{1/3}, SNFS (32/9)^{1/3} and t-dimensional sieving exactly (§5). An L[α] sampling method needs
    44	  candidates of log-size σ = 2α in the exponent: **σ = 1/2 for α = 1/4, 2/5 for 1/5, 1/5 for 1/10**, and
    45	  λ = O((log x)²/log log x) for polynomial time. In this model faster linear algebra (any ω ≤ 2) does not move the
    46	  cost.
    47	- **Scope of model S.** MNFS (shared rational filtering, then V fields on the survivors; L[β_a + v] columns), unions
