    66	| Tier | Sufficient condition (short) | Gives | Believed |
    67	|---|---|---|---|
    68	| E | sampling family with C* = O(F_α), α < 1/3 (rank-r aggregate, toric) | L[α], formally 1/4 | false for typical N (G_r) |
    69	| E | one-sided Bohr-set lister (smooth u, u mod N ≤ L[2α]) at L[α, O(1)] per output | L[α] (with u mod N ≤ y it is the doubly lister: poly time) | false |
    70	| E | doubly-smooth lister / S-unit enumeration / relation-lattice vectors / Cayley-graph navigation | heuristic poly time (navigation to arbitrary targets: rigorous randomized poly time) | false; in coset form it would also give prime-field DL |
    71	| E | short SLPs for Pochhammer polynomials (⇐ permanent in constant-free VP) | factoring ∈ P/poly, **rigorous** | false (τ-conjecture) |
    72	| E | P = NP ∩ coNP; BQP ⊆ BPP | P; BPP | false |
    73	| E | algebraic-group families whose annihilation separates CRT components with probability L_p[α]^{−O(1)} | L_p[α] | no structure known |
    74	| C | smooth numbers constructed in single short intervals near kN (Prop SI) | **1.2114** | open; a reformulation of one-sided Schnorr CVP (no reduction either way) |
    75	| C | complete search / sampler for smooth numbers in NFS rational rows | 1.6386 | open |
    76	| C | the same on the algebraic side | inf 0.7631 | open |
    77	| C | beyond-Johnson output-sensitive CRT list decoding; H_sel violations | c(θ) | open, implausible |
    78	| C | sparse F₂ linear algebra with ω < 2 | 1.8595 (MNFS only) | open, plausible-ish |
    79	| C | small-height polynomial pairs for general N | (32κ/9)^{1/3} | **false for almost all N** (GAE) |
    80	| C | Kirchner's ramified fields | 1.2114 (special N), 1.5263 (most N) | special: false for a.a. N; relaxed: unconstructed |
    81	| X | Coppersmith from a fraction f < 1/2 of p's bits | N^{f/2} (exponential) | open |
    82	
    83	**High-profile verdicts (§3).**
    84	- Matomäki–Radziwiłł / Harper-type existence of smooth numbers in short intervals: **irrelevant to speed** (no
    85	  algorithm, used by none).
    86	  They become the *existence half* of the promise-constructor route (Prop PC, Prop SI) only at the scale
    87	  y = L_X[1/3], h = L_X[2/3], far beyond current results.
    88	- Deterministic construction of a smooth number in a given interval: **sufficient for L[1/3, 1.2114]** at the Prop SI
    89	  scale, and **for heuristic polynomial time** in the Bohr-set form.
    90	- Polymath4 (primes): **unrelated**.
    91	- Sum-product: **unrelated**.
    92	- Expansion: existence of short paths is **unrelated**; constructive navigation is **sufficient** (= doubly listing).
    93	- Small-discriminant fields with a root mod N: **constant only**, and the height form is refuted for almost all N.
    94	- abc and S-unit counting: **unrelated**; algorithmic S-unit solving is **sufficient**.
    95	- Approximate CVP in prime lattices: a *method* for the listing targets; whether any approximation factor suffices is
    96	  schnorrcvp's question.
    97	- Coppersmith: **exponential regime only**.
    98	- CRT list decoding beyond Johnson: **constant only**, unless doubly.
    99	- Sparse linear algebra: **constant only**.
   100	- Factorial / Pochhammer straight-line programs: **sufficient (nonuniform P), and a landmark either way**.
   101	
   102	**Ranking (§4).**
   103	- *Most worth attacking (assessment; codex prefers C2 or C5 for clearer interfaces):* the Prop SI problem. Construct, or prove hard in a model, L[1/3]-smooth integers in intervals
   104	  of length L[2/3] near multiples of N. It is concrete, no barrier beyond method classes is known, it is
   105	  modulus-generic, and a solution would cut the factoring constant from 1.90 to 1.21.
   106	- *Landmark either way:* the straight-line complexity of factorials / Pochhammer polynomials. A proof of easiness gives
   107	  nonuniform polynomial-time factoring. A proof of hardness, quantified over power-of-two multiples, gives a
   108	  constant-free permanent lower bound (Bürgisser 2009).
   109	- *Highest-profile direct exponent target:* the doubly-smooth / Bohr-set lister (Schnorr's target). Its consequence for
   110	  prime-field DL (Prop DLT) makes it implausible.
   111	
   112	---
   113	
   114	## 1. Two principles
   115	
   116	### 1.1 Models
   117	
   118	**Sampling model S.**
   119	- From N, the algorithm generates candidates z from a family 𝓕 and *examines* them, paying ≥ 1 each.
   120	- Each z carries k = O(1) nonzero smoothness integers n₁(z), …, n_k(z), of total log-size λ(z) = Σ log|n_i(z)|.
   121	- Each z also carries a multiplicative link to Z/N: congruences of products, or ring maps as in resbarrier's LOM.
   122	  F₂-dependencies among B-smooth candidates therefore give X² ≡ Y² (mod N).
   123	- All candidates share **one factor base** of π(B)(1 + o(1)) columns, and each examined candidate is tested on *all*
   124	  its k integers, at cost ≥ 1. There is no cheaper pre-filtering on one component.
   125	- Linear algebra on K columns costs K^ω.
   126	
   127	**What S excludes (proof critic M1).**
   128	- MNFS: one shared rational side is sieved over E² points, and only its survivors are tested in V fields. The cost is
   129	  E²(1 + V·L[−r]), not E²·V, and there are L[β_a + v] columns.
   130	- Unions of M polynomial pairs: entropy 2 log E + log M with M separate factor bases. Their law is
   131	  2x/D + D(C − log M)/2 + O(log M), better than 2√(xC) for one pair.
   132	- The factory, which uses precomputation.
   133	- Listing (§1.3).
   134	Theorem SS therefore does not cover the best known algorithm (MNFS). Its role is to say exactly what a *single-family*
   135	sampling method must achieve.
   136	
   137	**Heuristic H_S.**
   138	- An examined z is B-smooth with probability ρ(λ(z)/log B)^{1+o(1)}, where −log ρ(u) = (1 + o(1))·u log u.
   139	- This is per examined candidate, independent of how z was selected. That clause is a **strengthening** of heurnfs H,
   140	  which only lets joint events multiply and so allows conditioning. The clause fails for candidates pre-filtered on one
   141	  component, which is why such filtering is excluded from S.
   142	- Smoothness tests and candidate generation cost L^{o(1)}.
   143	
   144	**Heuristic R.** π(B)·L^{±o(1)} relations are needed and suffice, and the dependencies are fruitful. For random-like
   145	relations, "needed" is Croot–Granville–Pemantle–Tetali 2012 (up to a constant). For structured families it is an
   146	assumption.
   147	
   148	**Size–entropy profile.**
   149	- C_𝓕(λ) := log #{z ∈ 𝓕 : λ(z) ≤ λ}.
   150	- The *law* λ(C) is its inverse: the smallest λ such that e^C candidates of size ≤ λ exist.
   151	- Example: two-polynomial NFS under G has λ(C) = 2√(xC) (heurnfs B1 with C = 2 log E). SNFS has √(2xC).
   152	
   153	**Why k = O(1) matters.** If a candidate's smoothness integers are many small pieces (k ≍ u), then ∏ρ(λ_i/μ) is no
   154	longer ρ(λ/μ). The extreme is "smooth by construction": a product of factor-base elements. That is the *listing* regime
   155	of §1.3, not a counterexample to §1.2. systeq §6, ptesplit and nonexact live there.
   156	
   157	### 1.2 Theorem SS (sampling) [model-PROVED in S + H_S + R; KNOWN in substance]
   158	
   159	Write T(λ) := √(2λ log λ).
   160	
   161	**(a) Fixed size [KNOWN].** If every examined candidate has size λ, the cost is exp((1 + o(1))T(λ)). In Pomerance's
   162	notation this is L(X)^{√2+o(1)} with X = e^λ, L(X) = exp(√(log X log log X)) (Pomerance 1982; 2008 survey "Smooth numbers
   163	and the quadratic sieve", MSRI Publ. 44). The optimum is attained at B = exp(T(λ)/2), with e^{T(λ)} candidates.
   164	
   165	**(b) L-units.** If λ = s·F_{2α}, the cost is L[α, 2√(αs)], with factor base L[α, √(αs)] and L[α, 2√(αs)] candidates.
   166	Equivalently: **L[α, c] needs candidates of total log-size ≤ (c²/(4α))·F_{2α}**, i.e. σ = 2α.
   167	
   168	**(c) Size–entropy law.** Let C*_δ := inf{C : C² ≥ (1 − δ)·2λ(C) log λ(C)} and C* := C*_0. Two regularity
   169	assumptions are used:
   170	- *Levels:* λ_max ≤ exp(x^{O(1)}), so that (1+ε)-adic size levels number L^{o(1)}.
   171	- *Stability:* C*_δ = (1 + o(1))C* as δ → 0, and λ((1 + o(1))C*) = (1 + o(1))λ(C*). This holds for every regularly
   172	  varying law, in particular every law in the catalogue.
   173	- Without stability the theorem fails: a step profile with λ = λ₀ up to just below T(λ₀), then huge, has C* huge while
   174	  cost ≈ e^{T(λ₀)} cannot be excluded under ρ^{1+o(1)} (proof critic m1).
   175	- (lower bound) Every algorithm in model S costs ≥ exp((1 − o(1))C*). This needs **no** assumption on linear algebra,
   176	  but it does use heuristic R (≈ π(B) relations are *needed*). H_S alone does not imply that: a generator could supply
   177	  relations with extra structure (codex). For *raw independent uniform integers* there are theorems on the
   178	  first-square-product threshold: Croot–Granville–Pemantle–Tetali 2012; Balister–Bollobás–Morris (arXiv 1608.03857,
   179	  Thm 1.1) give T(X) ∼ e^{−γ}·min_y π(y)X/Ψ(X, y). Their model allows dependencies through primes outside a prescribed
   180	  base, so they support R only as an analogy. For structured families R is an assumption.
   181	- (upper bound) If 𝓕 is enumerable at L^{o(1)} per candidate, H_S holds as an equality, and ω ≤ 2, the cost is
   182	  ≤ exp((1 + o(1))C*). This needs the population to extend a factor e^{o(C*)} beyond the threshold, which stability
   183	  guarantees, and yield concentration, which follows from H_S's independence by Chernoff. Without stability the
   184	  general statement is unjustified (codex suffmap_2 #4). The clean restricted theorem is for regularly varying laws,
   185	  in particular the power laws of (d).
   186	- So for single-family sampling, **ω < 2 does not help**. This agrees with heurnfs (GNFS stays at 1.9230 at ω = 1.5).
   187	  MNFS gains from ω < 2 only because its V fields have different column structures.
   188	
   189	**(d) Power laws.** For λ(C) = A·x^a(log x)^{1−a−b}·C^b with a > 0, 0 ≤ b < 2 and a < 2 − b, so that 0 < α < 1:
   190	
   191	  C* = (c + o(1))F_α,   α = a/(2 − b),   c^{2−b} = 4αA.
   192	
   193	The log-power 1 − a − b is the only one for which the law is L-homogeneous. Other log-powers move the constant to 0 or
   194	∞ at the same α. If b ≥ 2 there is no subexponential solution.
   195	
   196	**(e) Polynomial and quasi-polynomial time.** Here generation and tests must cost poly(x), not L^{o(1)}, and the
   197	level argument loses an additive O(log x) in C (proof critic m2). With these changes:
   198	- C* = O(log x) iff λ(C*) = O((log x)²/log log x).
   199	- λ = (log x)^k gives exp(O((log x)^{k/2}(log log x)^{1/2})), which is quasi-polynomial.
   200	
   201	**Proof.**
   202	1. *Optimal B for fixed λ.* With μ = log B and u = λ/μ, the relations needed are B, and each examined candidate succeeds
   203	   with probability ρ(u). So the number of candidates is ≥ exp(μ + u log u)·(1 − o(1)).
   204	   g(μ) := μ + (λ/μ)log(λ/μ) is minimised at μ₀ = √(λ log λ/2)(1 + o(1)). There log u = (½ + o(1))log λ, so
   205	   u log u = μ₀(1 + o(1)) and g(μ₀) = 2μ₀ = T(λ)(1 + o(1)).
   206	   Linear algebra costs B^ω = e^{ωμ₀} ≤ e^{2μ₀} when ω ≤ 2. This proves (a), and the ω-independence for fixed λ.
   207	2. *(b).* Substitute λ = sF_{2α}, μ = bF_α. Then u = (s/b)x^α(log x)^{−α}, log u = (α + o(1))log x, and
   208	   u log u = (αs/b)F_α. Minimising b + αs/b gives b = √(αs) and the value 2√(αs) = 2b.
   209	   This is `optimum_fixed_size`; exact for the tested rationals.
   210	3. *(c), lower bound.* The yield is Σ_{examined z} ρ(λ(z)/μ)^{1+o(1)} ≥ π(B)(1 − o(1)). Split the examined z into
   211	   (1+ε)-adic size levels, with ε → 0 slowly. Some level λ carries a 1/L^{o(1)} share of the yield.
   212	   - It contains ≤ e^{C_𝓕((1+ε)λ)} candidates.
   213	   - It requires ≥ exp(g(μ))·L^{−o(1)} ≥ exp((1 − o(1))T(λ)) examined candidates, by step 1, since sizes vary by a
   214	     factor ≤ 1 + ε.
   215	   - Hence C_𝓕((1+ε)λ) ≥ (1 − o(1))T(λ), and cost ≥ exp((1 − o(1))T(λ)).
   216	   - Minimising over the admissible λ: put C₁ = (1 − o(1))T(λ). Since λ(·) is nondecreasing, C_𝓕((1+ε)λ) ≥ C₁ gives
   217	     λ(C₁) ≤ (1+ε)λ, so C₁² ≥ (1 − o(1))·2λ(C₁) log λ(C₁). Hence C₁ ≥ C*_δ for some δ = o(1), and stability gives
   218	     cost ≥ exp((1 − o(1))C*). Here T((1+ε)λ) = (1 + O(ε))T(λ) was used.
   219	4. *(c), upper bound.* Take λ* = λ(C*), B = e^{T(λ*)/2}, and examine the e^{C*} candidates of size ≤ λ*. The expected
   220	   yield is ≥ e^{C*}ρ(λ*/μ) ≥ B·L^{−o(1)} (one more L^{o(1)} slack in C absorbs it). Linear algebra costs
   221	   B^ω ≤ B² = e^{T(λ*)} ≤ e^{C*}.
   222	5. *(d).* Substitute C = cF_α. Then λ = Ac^b x^{a+αb}(log x)^{1−a−αb}. With a + αb = 2α this is Ac^b F_{2α}, and
   223	   log λ = (2α + o(1))log x. So 2λ log λ = 4αAc^b F_α², while C² = c²F_α². Equality holds iff c^{2−b} = 4αA.
   224	   `verify_law_balance` checks the ratio is exactly 1 for every catalogued law.
   225	6. *(e).* C* = K log x iff 2λ log λ = K²(log x)², and log λ = (2 + o(1))log log x. ∎
   226	
   227	**Status.**
   228	- (a) is KNOWN (Pomerance's L(X)^{√2}). The size principle itself, and hence σ = 2α in L-units, is stated explicitly in
   229	  Pomerance 2008 (MSRI 44, eq. (8) and §"A general principle, a moral, and three bullets", p. 78): the analysis
   230	  "can serve, in an almost intact manner, for many factoring algorithms. What may change is the number X."
   231	- The matching exponent-level lower bound for random squares is Pomerance, ICM 1994 (Birkhäuser 1995, pp. 411–422),
   232	  sharpened to constants by CGPT 2012. The lemma number in the ICM paper is not checked.
   233	- The exponent bookkeeping α = (1+α)/2-type is heurnfs B5.
   234	- The fixed-point form c^{2−b} = 4αA, the ω-independence for single families, and (e) are our packaging. No prior
   235	  statement was found by us. They are routine.
   236	
   237	**What known methods look like under (d)** (`principle.LAWS`; every value exact):
   238	
   239	| Method | law λ(C) | (A, a, b) | α | c |
   240	|---|---|---|---|---|
   241	| Dixon | x | (1, 1, 0) | 1/2 | √2 |
   242	| QS | x/2 | (½, 1, 0) | 1/2 | 1 |
   243	| GNFS, any 2-poly selection under G | 2√(xC) | (2, ½, ½) | 1/3 | (64/9)^{1/3} = 1.9230 |
   244	| SNFS (κ = 1) | √(2xC) | (√2, ½, ½) | 1/3 | (32/9)^{1/3} = 1.5263 |
   245	| sieving dimension t = 3 | 2√(4xC/3) | (4/√3, ½, ½) | 1/3 | (256/27)^{1/3} = 2.1165 |

