45:  1. **Certificate decoders stop at the Johnson radius.** Take any Coppersmith / Howgrave-Graham / Boneh /
46-     Guruswami–Sahai–Sudan CRT list decoder: any dimension, any modulus built from factor-base prime powers, any
47-     weights. Within its guaranteed (Johnson-type) range, every certified output has a known divisor at least as
48-     large as the region searched.
49-     - Batching all those divisor lattices into one call is then worth only L^{o(1)}. This holds in the
50:       random-divisibility model on oblivious regions, for primitive points, in NFS scaling (the congruence
51:       instances are excluded) (Prop JR, model-PROVED).
52:     - So Boneh's L^{o(1)} is a barrier for that whole class, not an artifact of how Boneh used it.
53-     - Doing better needs output-sensitive listing beyond the Johnson radius. That would *be* a partial lister,
54-       i.e. the open problem itself.
55-  2. **Adaptive Θ(u)-tail elimination saves at most an L^{o(1)} factor in relations.** With boundedly many forced
56-     tail primes per relation, a single dependency still needs ≥ 2^{−8}/(kp) = L[β]^{1−o(1)} relations (Prop TAIL,
57-     under the strengthened randomness hypothesis H_tail⁺). What could still help:
58-     - sub-quadratic sparse linear algebra;
59-     - H_sel-type selection;
60-     - forcing by other means;
61-     - failure of H_tail⁺ itself.
62:  3. **For Π_β itself the target is extreme.** The congruence instances must cut the per-output cost from L[1 − β]
63-     (box sieve) to L[1/3, O(1)] even just to beat MNFS. Only the NFS-shaped listers have fixed-fraction
64-     thresholds.
65-- **smoothenum's crux H_sel is not equivalent to the primitive** (a draft claim, withdrawn after review). Its gain
66-  form H_sel^gain is exactly the hypothesis LQ uses. A violation of H_sel^gain with fresh primitive outputs, i.e. of
67-  H_sel by ≥ (M/U)·L[θ′r]^{−1+ε}, is a sufficient criterion for a speedup.
68-- **PTE O2.**
69-  - The naive three-term rigidity is false: explicit identities at M = 3 (exact) and M = 4 (constant remainder).
70-  - Even a true Lemma R-shaped version could not bound O2, because of Behrend-type parameter sets.
71-  - The right statement is a count. A nondegenerate pencil has ≤ (16/3)(cB^{1/2}(log B)^κ + 1) + min(2e − 2, 2B + 1)
72-    split members, height-free (Lemma PEN; codex proved it independently).
73-  - A pair of presentations with remainders of degree < k has ≤ C(2B+k, k)·(that) nondegenerate members (Prop
74-    O2-count). Degenerate W-pencils can add O(B²).
75-  - That is bounded amplification, not exclusion.
76-
