89-**Experiments (pre-registered, commit c78c6bc; every failure listed).**
90-- **E1.** P1, P2 and P5 held.
91-  - **P3 failed** at M = 4: no exact coprime small-c pencil with e = M.
92-  - **P4 failed as scored**: 2 flags, both G = 0 triples with moving degree 2. A disclosed post-hoc rescoring
93-    removes them.
94-  - Exact nondegenerate pencils carry ≤ 3 members up to M = 10.
95-- **E2** (c60 rational side). P6 (0 misses in 272 HG calls), P8 (≤ 0.77 certified outputs per call) and P9 held.
96-  - **P7 failed** in 1 of 8 cells (×2.37). Post hoc: non-primitive multiples of (30, 1). The primitive density
97-    1/(q + 1) fits.
98-  - Pooled Γ̂ ≈ 0.30: a call is worth less than one box point.
99-- **E3.** P10 and P11 held: legal adaptive strategies have median R*/n ≥ 0.917. The illegal oracle reaches
100-  0.79–0.95.
101-- One crash-and-fix deviation in E1 is disclosed.
102-
103-**Assessment (subjective).** Probability that Π_β is solvable for some β < 1/3: below 1% (ours); about 5% (codex,
104-blind). The results are conditional and method-level; they do not calibrate this number.
105-
106-**Where to push next.** An output-sensitive lister beyond the Johnson radius for the factor-base CRT code, i.e. for
107-the zero word f(t) mod q. Plain-RS capacity decoding (Brakensiek et al. 2026, as reported) shows that
108-beyond-Johnson decoding without auxiliary data is possible for RS codes. Whether anything like it exists for CRT
109-words, at vanishing rate and with output-sensitive huge lists, is the sharpest open form of the primitive.
