113-## 1. The canonical problem Π and its cost targets (Task 1)
114-
115-### 1.1 Definition
116-
117-**Π (split values of binary forms on a box).**
118-- *Input.* N, and data computable from N:
119-  - binary forms f₁, …, f_r ∈ Z[a, b] of degrees δ_i;
120-  - multiplicative bases 𝔛_i ⊂ Z, which are sets of integers: primes ≤ L[β], or shifted pieces such as m − a;
121-  - a box Ω ⊂ Z².
122-- *A witness* is a point z ∈ Ω such that every f_i(z) is 𝔛_i-split (a product of elements of 𝔛_i), together with
123-  the factorisations.
124-- *Task.* List R ≥ K^{1+o(1)} witnesses (K = Σ|𝔛_i|) at amortised cost τ per witness. Their parity vectors must
125-  have F₂-rank ≥ (1 − o(1))·#columns. Charge everything: building the forms and bases, setup, extraction, duplicate
126-  removal.
127-- An optional *window side* j allows |f_j(z)| ≤ V (bounded by construction) in place of f_j being split. The
128-  window side must then be smooth for the relation to be usable; this is the one-sided case.
129-
130-**The three primitives are instances [PROVED, elementary].**
131-
132-| Primitive | Forms and box | Bases |
133-|---|---|---|
134-| Π-nonexact (one-sided, View A at m = 0) | f₁(b, v) = bN + v on [1, U/N] × [−V, V]; side 2 is the window v | primes ≤ L[β] |
135-| Π-nonexact (View A, pieces m − a) / Π-split (ptecons item 3) | the same form bN + v | the shifted base {m − a : \|a\| ≤ B}. A split product ∏(m − a_l) is the lift P_A(m) of a split polynomial |
136-| Π-nonexact (doubly split) | the same form, with v also split | primes, or m − a |
137-| Π-NFS rational / algebraic / doubly | f₁ = a − bm (rational), f₂ = F(a, b) (homogeneous, degree δ) on [−E, E] × [1, E]; one side split, the other tested; or both split | primes / prime ideals |
138-
139:- *Why the congruence form is a binary linear form.* A product u = ∏x_a ≡ v (mod N), with |v| ≤ V and |u| ≤ U,
140-  is the same thing as a point (b, v) = ((u − v)/N, v) of the box at which bN + v is split. Conversely, every such
141-  point gives a product.
142-  - This is the bijection of smoothenum Prop GEN, here at the modulus N.
143-  - The rational NFS side is the same statement at the modulus m ≈ N^{1/(d+1)}.
144-- *View B.* Residues μ − a ∈ Z/N have no integer lift of size < N, so View B is the form bN + v with the base
145-  {μ̃ − a} of lifts. Its pieces are of size N; only the doubly-split version is meaningful.
146-- *Direction of the containments.*
147-  - Each primitive is an instance of Π: the forms, bases and box above define exactly its witness set.
148-  - The algebraic NFS side needs prime-ideal bookkeeping (norms factor; ideals are the columns). That is extra
149-    data in the factorisation, not a different listing problem.
150-  - No reduction in the other direction is claimed.
151-
152-**Scope.**
153-- Π is a listing problem only. Factoring also needs fruitful square dependencies, linear algebra and (one-sided)
154-  the smoothness of the window side (nonexact §6; heurnfs H; Croot–Granville–Pemantle–Tetali 2012).
155-- Literal listing costs ≥ 1 per output (codex). "Below one unit per output" means, here and in the task, below the
156-  box sieve's cost per output.
157-
158-### 1.2 Two regimes, one structural fact
159-
160:For the congruence instances the box is huge.
161-- To hold L[β] witnesses, bN + v must be split; it has size ≥ N, so its smoothness probability is
162-  L[1 − β]^{−Θ(1)}. Hence the box has area ≥ L[1 − β].
163-- So the box sieve costs **L[1 − β] per output**, while Π_β asks for **L[β, O(1)] per output**.
164-
165-For the NFS instances the box has area L[1/3, 2e] and the sieve costs L[1/3, r] per rational-smooth output.
166-
167-**Consequence [model-PROVED, heuristic H].**
168-- On the NFS instances, a fixed fractional saving of the box sieve's per-output cost beats MNFS: any θ > 0 on the
169-  rational side, and θ > θ* ≈ 0.03 / 0.022 on the algebraic / doubly side (numerical).
170:- On the congruence instances (Π_β proper), every target, *even only beating MNFS*, needs the per-output cost to
171-  drop from L[1 − β] to L[1/3, O(1)]. In the partial-listing language that is θ = 1 − O(F_{1/3}/F_{1−β}) → 1, i.e.
172-  essentially full output sensitivity.
173-- So, as a target and against the box-sieve baseline, Π_β demands far more than the NFS-shaped partial listers.
174-  This is a comparison of targets, not a proved complexity separation. Its natural home is the doubly-smooth lister
175-  of smoothenum T7/T8.
176-- The factoring consequence of either target also assumes random window smoothness and useful rank (codex #12).
177-
178-### 1.3 Cost targets
179-
180-**(A) Congruence instances, level α** (`targets.py`).
181-- Parameters: factor base L[α, κ]; window |v| ≤ L[2α, ν], i.e. log V = νF_{2α}; lister cost L[α, τ] per output.
182-- One-sided: v is smooth with probability L[α, −αν/κ]. The total exponent is
183-  - max(κ + τ + αν/κ, 2κ) one-sided;
184-  - max(κ + τ, 2κ) doubly split.
185-- Sanity check: α = 1/2, ν = 1, τ = 0 is Dixon's L[1/2, √2] (tested).
186-- The largest admissible τ for a target constant c [PROVED]:
187-  - τ_max = c − 2√(αν) if √(αν) ≤ c/2;
188-  - c/2 − 2αν/c otherwise;
189-  - doubly split: c (approached as κ → 0).
190-
191-**(B) NFS instances (MNFS program of heurnfs/smoothenum).**
192-- A partial lister enumerates its side's smooth pairs at cost E²·V·L[−θ·penalty].
193-- The penalties: r (rational), a (algebraic), r + a (doubly).
194-- c_rat(θ) uses the closed form of smoothenum/codex. c_alg and c_both are 5-variable Nelder–Mead programs with e ≤ 3
195-  *enforced as a constraint*, with a running minimum against MNFS. They are numerical upper bounds, not certified
196-  optima. The draft's cap was not enforced (codex #11); the values below are the corrected ones. The algebraic and doubly listers enumerate per field (cost includes V);
197-  rational outputs are tested in all V fields.
198-
199-**Table 1.1: what Π must cost, per output.**
200-
201-| Target | Congruence Π (Π_β, Π-split, Π-nonexact) | NFS-shaped Π |
202-|---|---|---|
203-| beat MNFS 1.9019 at α = 1/3 | one-sided: τ < 1.9019 − 2√(ν/3). Window ν = 1 needs per-output cost < L[1/3, 0.747] (ν = 0.5: 1.085; ν = 2: 0.269). Doubly split: any τ < 1.9019. Box sieve: L[2/3, Θ(1)] per output | rational: any θ > 0 (c = 1.8998, 1.8912, 1.8804, 1.8467, 1.786, 1.6386 at θ = .01, .05, .1, .25, .5, 1; slope −0.21). Algebraic: θ > θ*_alg (c = 1.8867, 1.8497, 1.7347, 1.5247, 0.8246 at θ = .05, .1, .25, .5, 1; 1.9019 at θ = .01). Doubly: θ > θ*_both (1.8745, 1.8253, 1.6711, 1.3868, 0.5732) |
204-| any α < 1/3 | τ = O(1) at level α with ν = O(1): per-output L[α, O(1)] against the sieve's L[1 − α] | doubly listing with 1 − θ ≲ F_α/F_{1−2α} → 0 (codex eqs. 8–10), together with the doubly-smooth side |
205-| α = 1/4 | L[1/4, c] iff τ + √ν < c (when √(ν/4) ≤ c/2). E.g. ν = 1/4 gives τ < c − 1/2 | 1 − θ ≲ (1/8)x^{−1/4}(log x)^{1/4} (codex eq. 10; θ ≥ 0.9923 at x = 10⁶) |
206-
207-- θ*_alg = 0.0303 and θ*_both = 0.0221 (bisection, 30 restarts, cap enforced). Codex's reduced program gives 0.0291
208-  and 0.0219. Below them, per-field enumeration costs more than MNFS's shared
209-  rational sieve.
210-- 0.8246 and 0.5732 are the e ≤ 3 values (codex: 0.8241 and 0.5716). The infima, attained only as e → ∞, are 0.7631 and 0 (smoothenum T6, T7).
