214-## 2. Barrier attempts (Task 2)
215-
216-### 2.1 What the crux is (Remark SEL; revised after both reviews)
217-
218-**Lemma (at most one primitive point per large lattice) [PROVED, elementary].**
219-- For a rational-side lattice Λ_{M,R} with M > 2E², all box points are integer multiples of one vector: two
220-  independent box vectors would span a parallelogram of area ≤ 2E² < M = det.
221-- So Λ_{M,R} contains at most one *primitive* box pair up to sign.
222-- Caution (codex, critic): Λ_M can be nonempty with **no** primitive box point. Example: E = 2, M = 12, R = 5,
223-  where (−2, 2) ∈ Λ but (−1, 1) ∉ Λ. "Nonempty" must mean "contains a primitive point" throughout.
224-
225-**Remark SEL [PROVED, elementary; the draft's "Prop EQ-SEL" equivalence is WITHDRAWN].**
--
240-
241-**What the natural models give** (codex blind round, checked):
242-- *Hidden fresh residues*: H_sel holds with C = 1 [model-PROVED]. But real roots are reductions of one public m,
243-  so the model hides the arithmetic the question is about.
244-- *Public unrestricted m*: a uniform H_sel is false (m = 1 + M with smooth M ≫ U puts (1, 1) in Λ_M). H_sel can
245-  only be an averaged statement.
246-- *Generic CRT/lattice manipulation with public residues*: a lower bound would be a superpolynomial lower bound
247-  for an explicit arithmetic search problem. We know no technique for that (assessment).
248-
249-**Conclusion.** The crux can be attacked only in method classes, or as a conditional statement. §2.2 does both for
250-the largest method class we found.
251-
252:### 2.2 Prop JR: Johnson-radius certificates batch lattices but gain only L^{o(1)} [model-PROVED; conditional]
253-
254-**Scope (restricted after review).**
255-- NFS scaling: the call's region lies inside the NFS box, so V ≤ U with log U = O(F_{1/3}) = O(log B).
256:- The congruence instances are **not** covered. There log V/log B → ∞, and a single call with P ≈ d ≈ V
257:  certifies one point with gain g(d) = L[1/3, Θ(1)] (critic M2). That is harmless for Table 1.1, because the
258-  per-output cost stays L[1 − β].
259-
260-**Certified outputs.** The class's output is *defined* as Cert = {z ∈ R : W(z) ≥ T}. Post-filtering is free;
261-uncertified returns are discarded, and the theorem says nothing about them. Uncertified returns are chosen
262-arithmetically (roots of h), so one could sit just below S with gain ≈ S^λ.
263-The draft let a call return an arbitrary superset; codex showed that permits Γ = V (codex #1, critic M5).
264-
265-**Decoder class 𝒟_J (weighted; unweighted is w ≡ 1).** Guruswami–Sahai–Sudan's weighted agreement, with
266-nonnegative weights w ≢ 0 on the primes of P = ∏q^{e_q} (prime powers allowed):
267-- W(z) = Σ_q J_q(z) w_q log q, where J_q = min(v_q(f(z)), e_q);
268-- certified iff W ≥ T := √(log V · Q), with Q = Σ e_q w_q² log q.
269:- The unweighted case w ≡ 1 is Boneh/GRS/HG: log V ≤ (log S)²/log P.
270-- GSS's actual theorem (FOCS 2000, Thm 4) has an extra tolerance √(… + ε z_max²) and guarantees inclusion, not
271-  "iff". Its moduli are pairwise coprime, with Guruswami–Sudan multiplicities.
272-- Our prime-power score J_q = min(v_q, e_q) is therefore a *GSS-type extrapolation*, not literally a GSS
273:  coordinate. Unweighted, it is covered by Boneh/HG in gcd form.
274-- Lattice dimension, shifts and multiplicities are arbitrary; only the guaranteed range enters.
275-
276-**Model H_div (stated after review).**
277-- *Regions.* Oblivious regions: rows or sub-boxes fixed before the call. Adaptive sequences of calls are allowed,
278-  with the hypotheses conditional on the transcript.
279-- *Points.* Primitive points only.
280-- *Divisibility.* For z uniform in R and q | P, the J_q are independent with Pr[J_q ≥ j] ≤ q^{−j}. The primitive
281-  density 1/(q^{j−1}(q + 1)) satisfies this (E2, §5.2).
282-- *Cofactor.* Given d(z) = d, the cofactor is smooth with probability ≤ P(y)·g(d), with g(d) ≤ d^λ (Dickman step).
283-- *What H_div excludes.* Arithmetic choice of the region. R = Λ_M ∩ box would violate it, and is H_sel itself
284-  (critic M1, codex #4). So **JR does not prove H_sel**. It proves that a Johnson-radius call, batching all
285-  divisor classes of P at once, cannot escape the H_sel-type count, *given* random divisibility on oblivious
286-  regions.
287-
288:**Lemma S≥V and Lemma CS [PROVED].**
289-- Every certified output has d ≥ V. Proof: by Cauchy–Schwarz, W² ≤ (Σ_agree w² log q)·log d ≤ Q·log d, and
290-  W² ≥ T² = Q log V.
291-- In the unweighted case, S ≥ V^D follows directly from (log S)² ≥ D log V·log P ≥ D log V·log S.
292-- Up to a factor of 2 between V and Coppersmith's radius X = (V − 1)/2.
293-
294:**Prop JR.** Assume NFS scaling, F := F_{1/3}, t = log V ≤ log U = O(F), λ ≤ 1/10 (true asymptotically), and
295-H_div. Then for every factor-base modulus P (any prime powers) and every nonzero weight vector,
296-
297-  Γ_cert ≤ V·E[d^λ·1_{W≥T}] ≤ exp( λt + O(F/log F + F^{0.9}log F + u + log log B) ) = L^{o(1)}.
298-
299-**Proof** (codex pilist_2's weighted proof, checked step by step by us; it replaces the draft's three-regime
300-proof, which the critic had found correct up to constants and which is kept in `decoder.py` as the numeric
301-Rankin bound).
302-1. Rescale w so that Q = t; the certified event becomes W ≥ t.
303-2. With s = 2(1 − λ), exponential Markov and independence give
304-   log Γ_cert ≤ t − st + Σ_q m_q(s w_q + λ), with m_q(a) = log E[q^{aJ_q}].
305-   Monotone domination by truncated geometric variables is legitimate because all exponents are ≥ 0.
306-3. *Heavy primes* H = {w_q > 1/4}. From Q = t, log P_H ≤ 16t. Also m_q(a) ≤ e_q(a − 1)₊ log q + log(e_q + 1),
307-   and (a − 1)₊ = (1 − λ)(2w − 1)₊ ≤ (1 − λ)w². So
308-   Σ_H m_q ≤ (1 − λ)Q_H + log τ(P_H) ≤ (1 − λ)t + O(F/log F).
309-4. *Light primes* (w_q ≤ 1/4): a ≤ 0.55.
310-   - Primes q ≤ Z = F^{1/4} contribute O(Z) uniformly in e_q.
311-   - For q > Z, Taylor-expand m_q around λ, using m_q′(λ) ≪ (log q)q^{λ−1} and m_q″(a) ≪ (log q)²q^{−0.4} for
312-     a ≤ 0.6:
313-     - base term Σ_{q≤B} m_q(λ) = O(u + log log B);
314-     - linear term O(√t), by Cauchy–Schwarz against Q = t;
315-     - quadratic term O(t(log Z)Z^{−0.4}) = O(F^{0.9} log F).
316-5. Sum: t − 2(1 − λ)t + (1 − λ)t = λt, plus O(F/log F) (divisors), O(F^{1/4}) (small light primes), O(√t)
317-   (linear), O(F^{0.9} log F) (quadratic) and O(u + log log B) (base). λt = O(λ log U) = O(log u). Every error term
318-   is O(u) or smaller (proof critic). ∎
319-
320-**Reading.**
321-- Lemma CS says every certified output has a known divisor at least the size of the region. Each divisor class
322-  then holds ≤ V/d ≤ 1 expected points, the H_sel-type count.
323-- The decoder's only freedom is to batch the τ(P) classes into one call. The proof shows batching is worth
324-  L^{o(1)} for every weight profile: heavy primes are capped by Q = t, and light primes contribute nothing beyond
325-  their mean.
326:- The unweighted Johnson-radius class (Boneh/GRS/HG) is the special case w ≡ 1.
327-
328-**Numbers (`src/pilist/sweeps.py`, the convex Rankin bound min_s V S^{−s}E[d^{s+λ}]).**
329-- Unweighted, z ≤ 10240 and powers ≤ 6: max log Γ = 3.13/4.87 (row/box) at 512 bits, 3.18/5.42 at 1024,
330-  3.98/4.07 at 4096, 4.53/−7.49 at 65536. Row maxima rise slowly (3.1 → 4.5). That is consistent with the proved
331-  exp(O(u + …)), which is L^{o(1)} but grows faster in u than smoothenum's (C u log u)^{v_box}.
332-- The maximiser is always log S ≈ log P ≈ log V: the single-lattice peak.
333-- Weighted, four families: max 5.39 at 1024 bits, < 0 at 65536.
334-- At E2 scale λ = 0.132 > 1/10, so E2's "JR bound" column is the numeric Rankin bound, not the theorem.
335-
336:### 2.3 Is Boneh's L^{o(1)} a barrier or an artifact?
337-
338-**A barrier, conditionally, for the Johnson-radius certificate class** (unweighted and weighted, any modulus,
339-prime powers, any dimension; model H_div; NFS scaling).
340:- It is not an artifact of Boneh's row-by-row use, of the choice of P, or of the weights.
341-- *Optimality of the method.* Chinburg–Hemenway–Heninger–Scherr (ASIACRYPT 2016) prove, by capacity theory, that
342-  Coppersmith's univariate full-modulus bound is optimal for auxiliary-polynomial certificates. This supports
343-  (J) as the method's real limit in the univariate case. It does **not** cover the divisor version or
344-  multivariate certificates (codex #15, critic M4). For those, (J) is the known guarantee, not a proved ceiling.
345-
346-**An artifact relative to beyond-Johnson listing.**
347-- A useful decoder must list every z in a region of size V ≤ U with B-smooth part ≥ y^{θ'}, using the whole
348-  factor base (log P ≍ B). That is agreement fraction log S/log P ≍ x^{2/3}/L[1/3]: far below Johnson, above
349-  capacity. Doing it output-sensitively is a partial lister with θ = θ' (Remark SEL (c)).
350-- *Literature status (corrected).* The draft's "every capacity-approaching decoder needs folded or derivative
351-  structure" is withdrawn.
352-  - Brakensiek–Chen–Putterman–Zhang–Zheng, "Algorithmic list decoding of Reed–Solomon codes up to capacity"
353-    (arXiv 2609.08005, Sept. 2026; preprint, not yet refereed; verified by the literature critic). It list-decodes
354-    *plain* RS codes over prime fields up to radius 1 − ε, at rates up to (1 − θ)ε, extended to all constant rates.
355-    The decoder gets no derivative information; Hasse derivatives are hidden formal variables.
356-  - Companion: Jeronimo, arXiv 2609.05870.
357-  - It needs a prime field. Lists have size n^{O_ε(1)} and time is q^{ε^{−O(1/θ)}}. As the agreement fraction
358-    → 0, here ≍ x^{2/3}/L[1/3], that exponent blows up superpolynomially. It is not a CRT decoder, so it does not
359-    transfer to our vanishing-agreement, huge-list, output-sensitive regime.
360-- *The known integer beyond-Johnson mechanism needs extra structure.* Cohn–Heninger, "Approximate common divisors
361-  via lattices" (ANTS X, 2013), the integer analogue of Parvaresh–Vardy, goes beyond the Johnson-type bound only for
362-  several correlated inputs sharing one divisor. Our setting has a single word f(z) mod P.
363-- No CRT or number-field extension of the hidden-derivative method was found (literature critic).
364-- Cohn–Heninger AMC 2015 Thm 1.3 is the number-field β²/d Johnson-type bound, not capacity (draft misclassified
365-  it; per codex).
366:- *Precursors.* Boneh 2002 §4: the CRT list-decoding bounds "are insufficient for improving the basic quadratic
367:  sieve". Boneh's Thm 3.2 already bounds the number of N ∈ I with gcd(N, S) > T, the per-class count used in JR.
368-  Guruswami's thesis §12.3.2 makes the same remark. No published L-power impossibility for the Johnson-radius
369-  class was found by us or the literature critic.
370-- *Chinburg et al.* say explicitly that the unknown-divisor version "appears to pertain to joint capacities of
371-  many adelic sets, a topic which has not been developed". The divisor/CRT optimality question is open.
372-- Bundling primes into blocks yields (n/w)(u/n)^w → 0 agreeing blocks (codex blind, eq. 17).
