428-### 3.3 Adaptive elimination with Θ(u) tail primes (Prop TAIL; restated after review)
429-
430-**Model H_tail⁺ (strengthened after codex #8).**
431-- Band columns are the primes in (B^{1−ε}, B], with ε = C/log u.
432-- A B-smooth value of size B^u has (1 − e^{−C} + o(1))·u band factors, counted with multiplicity (Dickman saddle
433-  point; HEURISTIC). The model assumes **exactly k** random band entries per relation; the bound is for row
434-  length exactly k, and odd unmatched configurations contribute 0.
435-- Each relation has ≤ j forced band entries chosen from the history. Lattice index ≤ U gives
436-  j ≤ log U/((1 − ε) log B) = O(1).
437-- Entry by entry: given the entire exposure history, including earlier entries of the same relation, each random
438-  entry equals any fixed prime with probability ≤ p := C′u log u/B^{1−ε} = L[β]^{−1+o(1)}.
439-- Without the entry-wise condition the bound fails: rows (Q, Q, Q′, Q′) always cancel (codex).
440-- *Relations with fewer than k band primes* (proof critic) cost only an extra e^{−Θ(u)} = L^{o(1)}, so they are
441-  not excluded. Discarding them, or keeping only relations whose band count is ≥ k, amounts to a factor base
442-  B^{1−ε} = L[β]^{1−o(1)} on the remaining columns. The conclusion R ≥ L[β]^{1−o(1)} is unchanged.
443-
444-**Prop TAIL [model-PROVED].** Under H_tail⁺, for any adaptive strategy (discards charged), and with
445-k ≥ 2j + 2:
446-
447-  Pr[some nonempty subset of the first R relations cancels on the band columns] ≤ Σ_{s=1}^{R} C(R, s)·Σ_{w≤js} C(ks, w)·(ks − w − 1)!!·p^{(ks−w)/2}
448-
449-and this is < 1/2 whenever **R ≤ 2^{−8}/(kp)**.
450-
451-**Proof.**
452-- If S cancels, the XOR of its random parts equals the XOR of its forced parts, of weight ≤ js. So ≥ (k − j)s/2
453-  disjoint position pairs carry equal primes.
454-- For a fixed S and pairing, the probability is ≤ p^{#pairs}: condition at the later entry of each pair.
455-- Union bound, with C(R, s) ≤ (eR/s)^s, C(ks, w) ≤ 2^{ks}, (n − 1)!! ≤ n^{n/2} and ksp ≤ 1:
456-  term_s ≤ (js + 1)·[e(R/s)·2^k·(ksp)^{(k−j)/2}]^s.
457-- With R ≤ c/(kp), ksp ≤ cs/R, and since (k − j)/2 ≥ 1, the bracket is ≤ e·2^k·c^{(k−j)/2}. Using
458-  k − j ≥ k/2 + 1, this is ≤ e√c·(2c^{1/4})^k.
459-- At c = 2^{−8} it is ≤ e·2^{−4−k}, and Σ_s (js + 1)(e2^{−4−k})^s < 1/2 for j ≤ k/2. ∎ (critic m1)
460-
461-**Constants.** c₀ = 2^{−8} is proved. The exact first-moment threshold gives R·k·p at the 1/2-level of:
462-- 2.48, 2.70, 2.72, 2.72 for j = 0, k = 4, 8, 16, 32;
463-- 0.64 → 2.34 for j = 1, k = 4 → 64;
464-- 0.55 → 2.11 for j = 2, k = 6 → 64;
465-- 0.52 at (16, 6) (critic).
466-The draft's "c(k, j) → 1, ≈ 0.8–0.9" was wrong.
467-
468-**Prior art.** The non-adaptive case is classical: k ≥ 3 random entries per row need Θ(n) rows (Calkin 1997;
469-Balakin–Kolchin–Khokhlov 1991; Pittel–Sorkin 2016). Croot–Granville–Pemantle–Tetali (Annals 2012, §8.2) show that
470-unlimited large primes cut the stopping time only by a constant factor. Prop TAIL's contribution is the adaptive,
471-entry-wise-conditional version with j forced entries. No prior statement of that version was found by us.
472-
473-**Consequence (narrowed after review).**
474-- Under LQ and H_tail⁺, bounded forcing cannot reduce the number of relations needed for one dependency below
--
