769-## 6. Where the L[1/3] limit bites (synthesis; an interpretation, not a proved identification)
770-
771:1. **Π_β itself (congruence form).** As a target, even beating MNFS requires the per-output cost to fall from the
772-   box sieve's L[1 − β] to L[1/3, O(1)]. That is essentially full output sensitivity. Only NFS-shaped listers admit
773-   fixed-fraction gains (Table 1.1).
774-2. **Box access in general.** The bite is a selection hypothesis. H_sel^gain is exactly what LQ needs, and an
775-   H_sel^gain violation with fresh primitive outputs, i.e. a violation of H_sel by ≥ (M/U)·L[θ′r]^{−1+ε}, would be
776-   an improvement (Remark SEL). We do *not* claim the two are equivalent.
777-3. **Certificate decoders.**
778-   - Conditionally on random divisibility on oblivious regions, for primitive points and in NFS scaling, the
779-     Johnson radius is where the limit bites for the
780:     whole Coppersmith/Boneh/GSS class: certified ⇒ d ≥ V, and batching gains L^{o(1)} (Prop JR; E2 Γ̂ ≈ 0.3).
781-   - The range of the method is proved optimal only for the univariate full-modulus case (Chinburg et al.).
782-4. **Adaptive Θ(u) tails.** Conditionally on H_tail⁺, the relation count cannot shrink (Prop TAIL). What remains
783-   is sparse LA with ω < 2 (heurnfs (b)), H_sel-type forcing, and the randomness hypothesis itself.
784-5. **PTE O2.** Counts, not rigidity: Lemma PEN and Prop O2-count give bounded amplification. Measured exact
785-   pencils carry ≤ 3 members.
786-
787-Items 2–4 share one *form*: certifying a known factor-base divisor larger than the region searched should cost
788-about one unit per region-sized lattice. Each result proves this form only inside its own model or method class.
