    41	- **The cleanest new result is Theorem J (PROVED, averaged over the interval position).** Take any Coppersmith /
    42	  Howgrave-Graham / Boneh / Guruswami–Sahai–Sudan CRT list decoder:
    43	  - any modulus P built from factor-base prime powers, any weights, any lattice dimension;
    44	  - univariate, or bivariate batched over many multipliers k.
    45	  Averaged over where the window sits, one call returns at most L[2/3, −2/(3κ) + o(1)] smooth certified integers.
    46	  That is no more than testing L[2/3]^{o(1)} random integers. The trivial "divisor trick" z = d·⌈x/d⌉ attains this
    47	  bound at leading order, so the whole Johnson-radius class is no better than the trivial trick.
    48	  - The bound needs no heuristic: averaging over x replaces the random-divisibility assumption. It holds whenever
    49	    the modulus is chosen without looking at x (or from a family fixed in advance of size L[2/3]^{o(1)}).
    50	  - It does not cover moduli selected from x. Making that selection is the problem itself.
    51	- **Consequences beyond factoring (Prop DLI).** A constructor that works for every modulus would also give
    52	  prime-field discrete logarithms in L_p[1/3, ≤ 3^{1/3} = 1.442] (heuristic). Collecting relations costs 1.2114.
    53	  The constructor also handles the descent of medium-sized primes, at L[1/3, 0.99] when queried with the modulus
    54	  ⌊jp/q⌋. The classical ECM "booting" step, at 3^{1/3}, then dominates. Prime-field DL has been stuck at 1.902, so a
    55	  modulus-generic constructor would break **two** problems believed hard.
    56	  - No implication was found for class groups or for other groups.
    57	  - It would also give the first subexponential-time constructions of x^ε-smooth integers in [x, x + √x]. That is
    58	    the constructive counterpart of the Matomäki–Radziwiłł existence theorem; none is known to us.
    59	- **The converse fails in the black-box model.** A factoring oracle does not help: in that model finding a smooth
    60	  integer in a window needs ≥ 1/δ smoothness tests, where δ is the local density (Lemma BB). The constructor's
    61	  difficulty is *selection*, not *testing*.
    62	- **NP-hardness does not reach Prop SI's scale** (§6).
    63	  - "Smooth over an explicitly given prime list S, in an interval" is NP-complete under randomized reductions
    64	    (Prop XS; parsimonious, from exact cover by 3-sets).
    65	  - For the real problem (all primes ≤ y) no NP-hardness is known in any range.
    66	  - It is provably not NP-hard unless NP = coNP (via NP ∩ coNP or TFNP) in three cases:
    67	    - when h ≤ poly(log x);
    68	    - in every range where all intervals provably contain a smooth integer;
    69	    - at Prop SI's own scale, if the existence half of Prop SI holds.
    70	  - Unconditionally, the interval sieve solves Prop SI's scale in L[2/3]. So a reduction from 3SAT would need
    71	    output size ≥ m^{3/2−o(1)} unless ETH fails.
