   102	
   103	## 1. The problem, and what counts as a barrier
   104	
   105	- **SMOOTH-INT(x, h, y).** Given x, h, y, output a y-smooth integer in [x, x + h], or (decision) say whether one
   106	  exists. Prop SI asks for it at scale (h, y) = (L_x[2/3, ν], L_x[1/3, κ]) with ν > 2/(3κ), at x = kN, in
   107	  L^{o(1)} time, or in L[1/3, τ] time with τ < 0.757.
   108	- **Lemma EQ (PROVED).** If a constructor works for every modulus M and every k ≤ L[1/3, C], then calling it with
   109	  M := x, k := 1 solves SMOOTH-INT(x, L_x[2/3, ν], L_x[1/3, κ]) for every x. Conversely an arbitrary-interval
   110	  constructor serves the intervals I_k = [kN, kN + h]. ∎
   111	  - The equivalence needs uniformity in the modulus. A constructor that uses the shape of N (for example that N is a
   112	    semiprime) is not covered. No such mechanism is known.
   113	- **The trivial costs.**
   114	  - Sieving [x, x + z] costs ≍ z log log y + u·y (Granville 2008, §5.7; KNOWN, checked in the text). One output
   115	    needs z ≈ 1/ρ(u₀) = L[2/3, 2/(3κ)].
   116	  - Granville notes that the y-term is a likely lower bound when the primes ≤ y are part of the input, and that
   117	    "one might guess that it is not necessary to have z in the running time". Prop SI asks exactly for that. The
   118	    π(y) term does not bound the cost per call, because the factor base is shared across calls.
   119	- **What a barrier could be.** An unconditional superpolynomial lower bound for an NP search problem implies P ≠ NP
   120	  (suffmap §4). So barriers here are of three kinds:
   121	  - (i) model lower bounds (§5);
   122	  - (ii) hardness reductions, with their limits (§6);
   123	  - (iii) consequences that would break other problems believed hard (§2).
